  Okay, I''m Logos I, ready to dive into this "Neural Activity Forecasting" task on ZAPBench. I''ve just absorbed the task description, and my mind is racing. This is a fascinating problem!


  First things first, I''m a recursive agent, and I need to understand my objective clearly.  The goal is to predict 32 timesteps of zebrafish brain activity, which means I''ll be working with a massive dataset of 71,721 neurons.  That''s a lot of data! I see the input shape is `(batch_size, 4, 71721)` and the output needs to be `(batch_size, 32, 71721)`. And of course, the metric is Mean Negative MAE across three runs, where lower is better.


  I''m also paying close attention to the constraints.  The 24GB GPU memory limit is a major factor. I''ll need to be mindful of model size and memory usage.  The restriction against pretrained models is interesting; it means I''ll be starting from scratch, focusing on my own architectural innovations.  And I must use the provided training framework, specifically modifying `submission.py` - that''s my sandbox.


  Now, that "Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline" (ID 1) catches my eye. The fact that it treats each neuron independently is highlighted as a "major limitation." That''s a huge clue!  It screams that exploring relationships between neurons, spatial or temporal, is where I should focus my research.


  My current plan is pretty straightforward.  Before I even *think* about building my own model, I need to understand the playing field.  Step one: *deep dive* into that baseline model. I need to see the code, the hyperparameters, and most importantly, the exact results. It''s the logical starting point.


  Second, I need to understand the foundation: I will review `defaults.py`.  This will give me the under-the-hood workings of the framework and what I''m overriding in `submission.py`.


  Once I''ve grasped the baseline and the framework, then I''ll formulate a hypothesis. Given the hint, it''s very likely that modeling neuron interactions will be my starting point.


  So, let''s get started.  I need to first execute the actions I''ve set out for myself. First, I need to: `/execute_action{review 1}` - I will thoroughly examine the baseline submission. And then: `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}` - I need to examine the default implementations.

  '
