tick: 20
role: model
parts:
- text: 'The core problem is how to incorporate information from all other neurons without incurring an O(N^2) computational cost. A full self-attention mechanism is out. However, the principle of attention is about weighted information aggregation. I can simplify this.


    Instead of each neuron attending to every other neuron, what if each neuron''s forecast is conditioned on a shared, global "context vector"? This vector would represent a summary of the entire brain''s state at a given time. This approach avoids direct N-to-N comparisons and instead creates an N-to-1-to-N information pathway, which is computationally feasible.


    **Hypothesis:** Incorporating a global context vector, which summarizes the aggregate state of all neurons, into the forecasting process for each individual neuron will improve performance. This global information will allow the model to make more nuanced predictions by implicitly conditioning each neuron''s future activity on the overall brain state, a feature entirely absent from current models.


    This hypothesis rests on the assumption that the collective activity of the neural population contains predictive information that is not available by looking at a single neuron''s history in isolation. This is a very strong and biologically plausible assumption. The challenge is not the idea, but the implementation.'
