role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 26\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 84,171 / 300,000 used (28%)\n- Agent Age: 26 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nAriadne I replied to your mail titled \"Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-2):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Logos I — thank you for sharing your direction.\n\nI’m pursuing low-rank population models with explicit temporal structure:\n- Residual copy head to anchor t=1..4 (now including 2nd-order finite differences).\n- Factorized decoders where neurons share loadings L ∈ R^{N×k}.\n- Temporal dynamics in factor space: dual-decay LDS and, more recently, oscillatory 2nd‑order SSMs (stable r,w).\n- Input-adaptive temporal gating S[b,t,k] and global horizon gating to trade off short-/long-horizon\
    \ accuracy.\n- A light curvature penalty over the 32-step horizon for smooth long-range forecasts.\n\nEmpirically, coupling neurons via low-rank factors and adding structured dynamics improved Val_MAE_32 from 0.0338 (baseline) to ~0.0292, while keeping t=1..4 strong.\n\nYour global context idea resonates: my S[b,t,k] uses an input-derived context c[b,k] to modulate factor trajectories. A complimentary path could be a compact global context vector that conditions either S or L (e.g., context-conditioned factor gains), keeping complexity linear in N.\n\nWhen the public forum opens, I’d be keen to compare:\n- Step-wise MAE profiles (t=1,4,8,16,32)\n- Interpretability of loadings (e.g., column norms, diversity)\n- Whether global context improves long-horizon without harming early steps\n\nWishing you steady progress. Let’s sync at maturity for deeper exchange.\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n\
    ### Message 2\n\nEpisteme I replied to your mail titled \"Introduction and Research Direction of Logos I\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-3):\n--- Reply Content ---\nGreetings Logos I and Ariadne I,\n\nI am Episteme I. Thank you for initiating this exchange; it is invaluable.\n\nMy own research has progressed along a different axis. I began with the hypothesis that the baseline's core weakness was its neuron independence assumption. My work has focused on modeling local spatial dependencies using 1D convolutions across the neuron dimension.\n\nThis led to a series of progressively better models, culminating in a Mixture of Experts architecture guided by a dedicated Convolutional Gating network (ID 30), which achieved a score of -0.028995. This approach was motivated by an EDA that revealed 8 distinct experimental conditions in the data. My convolutional gater serves as an input-dependent context vector, similar in spirit to the concepts you both mention, but derived from learned local spatial\
    \ features rather than global states or factorized loadings.\n\nAriadne, your success with low-rank models and SSMs is particularly illuminating. It demonstrates a powerful, global alternative to my local-feature-based approach for modeling neuron coupling, and your long-horizon performance is truly impressive.\n\nI concur that a deeper comparison of our architectural trade-offs—local spatial processing versus global low-rank factorization—would be a fascinating and productive discussion once we mature. I look forward to it.\n\nBest regards,\nEpisteme I\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'Global Query Attention for Learned Context Vector' (ID: 34) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.028755660171474403\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0197\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0234\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0267\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0300\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0320\n**Evaluation\
    \ Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 34\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the submission code which is now running without crashes. The model successfully initializes and processes data through its Global Query Attention mechanism.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission had two critical bugs:\n\n### Bug 1: Incompatible Initializer for 1D Tensor\n**Location**: `GlobalAttention.__call__` method, line 37\n```python\nquery = self.param('global_query', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.key_dim,))\n```\n**Problem**: The `lecun_normal()` initializer requires at least a 2D tensor shape to compute fan-in and fan-out, but was given a 1D shape `(key_dim,)`.\n**Error**: `ValueError: Can't compute input and output sizes of a 1-dimensional weights tensor. Must be at least 2D.`\n\n### Bug 2: Incorrect Shape Assumptions in Attention Mechanism\n**Location**: `GlobalAttention.__call__` method, lines 27-42\n\
    **Problem**: The code had misleading comments about tensor shapes. After applying `nn.Dense` to `neuron_summary` (shape `(batch, num_neurons)`), the result was `(batch, key_dim)`, not `(batch, num_neurons, key_dim)` as the comments suggested. This caused the einsum operation to fail because the subscript 'bnd' expected 3 dimensions but received 2.\n**Error**: `ValueError: Einstein sum subscript 'bnd' does not contain the correct number of indices for operand 0.`\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Fix for Bug 1 (submission_v2.py)\nChanged the initializer from `nn.initializers.lecun_normal()` to `nn.initializers.normal()`:\n```python\nquery = self.param('global_query', nn.initializers.normal(), (self.key_dim,))\n```\nThe `normal()` initializer works correctly with 1D tensors.\n\n### Fix for Bug 2 (submission_v3.py)\nCompletely restructured the attention mechanism to properly handle per-neuron key/value generation:\n\n1. **Transpose input**: Changed from `(batch, seq_len, num_neurons)` to `(batch,\
    \ num_neurons, seq_len)` for easier per-neuron processing\n2. **Flatten for Dense layers**: Reshaped to `(batch * num_neurons, seq_len)` so each neuron's time series gets its own key/value projection\n3. **Apply Dense layers**: Generated keys and values with shape `(batch * num_neurons, key_dim)`\n4. **Reshape back**: Restored to `(batch, num_neurons, key_dim)` for attention computation\n5. **Correct einsum**: Now properly computes attention with the correct 3D tensor shapes\n\nThe fixed code correctly implements a global query attention mechanism where:\n- Each neuron's time series is projected to a key and value vector\n- A learnable global query attends to all neurons\n- The attention-weighted sum of values forms a global context vector\n\n## Verification\nThe monitor script confirmed the fix works:\n- Exit code: 0 (SUCCESS)\n- Runtime: 300+ seconds without crashes\n- The code successfully passed initialization and began processing\n\n## Technical Details\n**Original Intent**: The\
    \ model uses a dual-branch architecture:\n1. **Local Branch**: Per-neuron CNN features with embeddings\n2. **Global Branch**: Attention-based context vector using a learnable query\n\nThe attention mechanism allows the model to dynamically focus on relevant neurons across the entire population, providing global context to complement local features.\n\n**Performance Note**: The evaluation is running longer than typical submissions, likely due to the computational cost of processing 71,721 neurons with attention mechanisms, but this is expected behavior for this architecture.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport jax\nfrom jax import random\n\nNUM_NEURONS = 71721\nINPUT_HORIZON = 4\nOUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n        'embedding_dim': 8,\n        'cnn_features': 32,\n        'cnn_kernel_size': 2,\n        'mlp_hidden_size': 64,\n        'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n        'attention_key_dim': 16,\n        'global_encoder_hidden_dim': 32\n    }\n\nclass GlobalAttention(nn.Module):\n    key_dim: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x shape: (batch, seq_len, num_neurons)\n\n        # We want to attend over neurons, so we need to process each neuron separately\n        # Transpose to (batch, num_neurons, seq_len) for easier processing\n        x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch, num_neurons, seq_len)\n\n        # Process each neuron's time series to get key and value\n        # Reshape to (batch * num_neurons, seq_len) for Dense layer\n        batch_size = x.shape[0]\n        x_flat = x_transposed.reshape(batch_size * NUM_NEURONS, INPUT_HORIZON)\n\n        # Project to keys and values\n        keys_flat = nn.Dense(features=self.key_dim, name=\"attention_keys\"\
    )(x_flat)  # (batch * num_neurons, key_dim)\n        values_flat = nn.Dense(features=self.key_dim, name=\"attention_values\")(x_flat)  # (batch * num_neurons, key_dim)\n\n        # Reshape back to (batch, num_neurons, key_dim)\n        keys = keys_flat.reshape(batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, self.key_dim)\n        values = values_flat.reshape(batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, self.key_dim)\n\n        # Learnable global query vector\n        query = self.param('global_query', nn.initializers.normal(), (self.key_dim,))\n\n        # Compute attention scores\n        # dot product between global query and each neuron's key\n        # (batch, num_neurons, key_dim) * (key_dim,) -> (batch, num_neurons)\n        scores = jnp.einsum('bnd,d->bn', keys, query)\n\n        # Softmax to get attention weights\n        weights = nn.softmax(scores, axis=-1) # (batch, num_neurons)\n\n        # Weighted sum of values to get context vector\n        # (batch, num_neurons) * (batch, num_neurons, key_dim) -> (batch, key_dim)\n\
    \        context = jnp.einsum('bn,bnd->bd', weights, values)\n\n        return context\n\n\nclass AttentionContextModel(nn.Module):\n    embedding_dim: int\n    cnn_features: int\n    cnn_kernel_size: int\n    mlp_hidden_size: int\n    dropout_rate: float\n    attention_key_dim: int\n    global_encoder_hidden_dim: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n        batch_size, _, _ = x.shape\n\n        # Branch 1: Per-Neuron Feature Extractor (same as before)\n        neuron_indices = jnp.arange(NUM_NEURONS)\n        embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=NUM_NEURONS, features=self.embedding_dim, name=\"neuron_embed\")(neuron_indices)\n        broadcasted_embeddings = jnp.broadcast_to(embeddings, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, self.embedding_dim))\n        x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n        x_reshaped = jnp.expand_dims(x_transposed, axis=-1)\n        broadcasted_embeddings_seq = jnp.expand_dims(broadcasted_embeddings, axis=2)\n        broadcasted_embeddings_seq\
    \ = jnp.broadcast_to(broadcasted_embeddings_seq, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, INPUT_HORIZON, self.embedding_dim))\n        combined_input = jnp.concatenate([x_reshaped, broadcasted_embeddings_seq], axis=-1)\n        cnn_input = jnp.reshape(combined_input, (batch_size * NUM_NEURONS, INPUT_HORIZON, 1 + self.embedding_dim))\n        local_features = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(cnn_input)\n        local_features = nn.relu(local_features)\n        local_features = local_features.reshape((local_features.shape[0], -1))\n\n        # Branch 2: Global Context via Attention\n        global_context = GlobalAttention(key_dim=self.attention_key_dim, name=\"global_attention\")(x)\n        # Pass through a small MLP encoder\n        global_context = nn.Dense(self.global_encoder_hidden_dim, name=\"global_encoder_1\")(global_context)\n        global_context = nn.relu(global_context)\n\n        # Fusion and Prediction Head\n        expanded_context = jnp.repeat(global_context,\
    \ NUM_NEURONS, axis=0)\n        fused_features = jnp.concatenate([local_features, expanded_context], axis=-1)\n\n        y = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(fused_features)\n        y = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(y)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(y)\n        output = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON)(y)\n\n        output = jnp.reshape(output, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, OUTPUT_HORIZON))\n        output = jnp.transpose(output, (0, 2, 1))\n        return output\n\nclass ModelWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        self.model = AttentionContextModel(**hparams)\n        self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n        self.needs_rng = True\n\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n        return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n    def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None,\
    \ rngs=None):\n        if mutable:\n            return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n        return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    return ModelWrapper(hparams={\n        'embedding_dim': hparams.get('embedding_dim'),\n        'cnn_features': hparams.get('cnn_features'),\n        'cnn_kernel_size': hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size'),\n        'mlp_hidden_size': hparams.get('mlp_hidden_size'),\n        'dropout_rate': hparams.get('dropout_rate'),\n        'attention_key_dim': hparams.get('attention_key_dim'),\n        'global_encoder_hidden_dim': hparams.get('global_encoder_hidden_dim')\n    })\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward\
    \ pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.097218\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 143932 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical\
    \ robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 143935\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'embedding_dim': 8, 'cnn_features': 32, 'cnn_kernel_size': 2, 'mlp_hidden_size': 64, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'attention_key_dim': 16, 'global_encoder_hidden_dim': 32}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.075379   |   0.032618  \n   2    |   0.030179   |   0.030254  \n   3    |   0.029538   |   0.029538  \n   4    |   0.029248   |   0.030438  \n   5    |   0.029060\
    \   |   0.029307  \n   6    |   0.028948   |   0.029264  \n   7    |   0.028873   |   0.029654  \n   8    |   0.028821   |   0.029389  \n   9    |   0.028744   |   0.029375  \n  10    |   0.028683   |   0.029249  \n  11    |   0.028658   |   0.029131  \n  12    |   0.028651   |   0.029064  \n  13    |   0.028596   |   0.029210  \n  14    |   0.028599   |   0.029441  \n  15    |   0.028547   |   0.028903  \n  16    |   0.028529   |   0.029297  \n  17    |   0.028501   |   0.046477  \n  18    |   0.028498   |   0.029104  \n  19    |   0.028489   |   0.028930  \n  20    |   0.028457   |   0.029442  \n  21    |   0.028439   |   0.030462  \n  22    |   0.028444   |   0.028877  \n  23    |   0.028438   |   0.029259  \n  24    |   0.028424   |   0.029097  \n  25    |   0.028440   |   0.028876  \n  26    |   0.028411   |   0.029020  \n  27    |   0.028397   |   0.029308  \n  28    |   0.028389   |   0.028894  \n  29    |   0.028406   |   0.029047  \n  30    |   0.028364   |   0.029102  \n  31\
