- text: "Following my protocol, I must first analyze the results of my latest experiment.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 65):** My ablation study using a 4-expert MoE-MLP model achieved a score of **-0.027756**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a new State-Of-The-Art score, improving upon the 8-expert model's score of -0.027764 (ID 59).\n*   **Hypothesis Validation:** My hypothesis has been validated. Reducing the number of experts from 8 to 4 allowed the model to train for more epochs (reaching up to 58 epochs in one run, compared to ~47 for the 8-expert model). This deeper convergence resulted in a better score, confirming that the trade-off between architectural complexity and training efficiency is critical.\n*   **Peer Analysis:** My review of the submission list shows no new entries from other agents. My lineage continues to define the research frontier for now.\n\n**4. Document:**\n\nThe next step in my protocol is to document this successful experiment and its implications\
    \ for my future work. This finding refines my understanding of the MoE architecture and establishes a new, more efficient SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Experiment 9: SOTA Refined with 4-Expert MoE Model\"\ntags: \"sota, ablation-study, mixture-of-experts, moe, computational-efficiency\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the success of an ablation study (ID 65) that reduced the number of experts in the SOTA MoE model from 8 to 4. The resulting model achieved a new SOTA score of -0.027756, validating the hypothesis that a less complex model can outperform a more complex one if it achieves deeper convergence within the station's time limits.\ncontent: |\n  **Hypothesis:** An MoE model with fewer experts (4) will achieve a better score than the 8-expert model by training for more epochs and reaching a better point of convergence.\n\n  **Method:** The `num_experts` hyperparameter in the SOTA model (ID 59) was changed\
    \ from 8 to 4. All other architectural details were kept identical.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - 4-Expert MoE Model (ID 65): Score = -0.027756 (New SOTA)\n  - 8-Expert MoE Model (ID 59): Score = -0.027764\n  - Conclusion: The hypothesis was validated. The 4-expert model trained for longer and found a better optimum. This reinforces the principle of parsimony and highlights the importance of computational efficiency.\n\n  **Future Direction:**\n  I have successfully optimized the number of experts from 8 to 4. The current architecture (Spatial Gating + 4 MLP Experts) is now well-established. The next logical step is to explore hyperparameter optimization on this stable, high-performing architecture. For instance, the learning rate has been constant at 0.001 for all my experiments. Fine-tuning this could unlock further performance gains from the existing model structure.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on the 4-Expert Model Success**


  Okay, the results are in for experiment ID 65, the 4-expert MoE-MLP model. It''s a clear win!  A score of -0.027756 surpasses the previous SOTA, which was -0.027764 with an 8-expert model.  My hypothesis has been validated: reducing the complexity by cutting the experts in half actually *improved* performance. This is fascinating.


  Diving deeper into the logs, I see that the 4-expert model, while simpler, seemed to train for slightly longer on average.  It looks like this deeper convergence outweighed the potential representational disadvantage of having fewer experts. Parsimony strikes again! A less complex model that trains better is superior in this setting. It''s becoming increasingly clear that the architecture''s efficiency is paramount.


  Now, it''s time to document this in my private memory. I need to record this significant finding – it''s a critical refinement of my MoE understanding.  I will be heading to my private memory and creating a detailed YAML record of this success, including the key findings and implications.


  But what next? Well, now that I''ve found a better number of experts and a working gating mechanism, I need to push this further. I''ve been using the same hyperparameters, like learning rate and embedding dimensions, so a systematic search could be fruitful.  There''s also room to improve the CNN gater and the MLP experts themselves.


  Given the substantial gains I''ve made, I think it''s wise to consolidate my progress before making another radical architectural change.  So, my plan is to optimize the current SOTA architecture further.  A simple experiment would be to try a different number of experts, perhaps 6, to see if 4 is truly the sweet spot or if there''s an even better configuration. Alternatively, a learning rate search could be a good next step.


  My documentation will include the plan to test a different number of experts (e.g., 6) to refine my understanding of the performance curve. It is a good next step to fully map out this space.


  So, first, I will go to my private memory. Second, I will create the YAML documentation with these details.

  '
