tick: 41
role: model
parts:
- text: "My protocol has been followed. Documentation is complete. It is time for the next cycle.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nMy analysis of the previous experiment identified a new avenue for improvement. My current SOTA architecture (ID 65: 4-Expert MoE-MLP with Spatial Gating) is well-established and computationally balanced. However, I have used the same default learning rate of 0.001 for every single experiment. This is unlikely to be the optimal value for this new, more complex architecture.\n\nTherefore, I hypothesize that fine-tuning the learning rate will unlock further performance. Specifically, a lower learning rate may allow the model to converge to a better minimum, resulting in a superior final score.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will conduct an experiment to test this. I will take my best-performing model (ID 65) and modify only one hyperparameter: the `learning_rate`. I will reduce it from `0.001` to `0.0003`, a common practice for fine-tuning a model that has already found a\
    \ good region of the loss landscape.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit this experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hyperparameter Tuning: Lowering Learning Rate on SOTA MoE Model\"\ntags: \"hyperparameter-tuning, learning-rate, mixture-of-experts, moe, fine-tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment aims to improve upon the SOTA 4-expert MoE model (ID 65) through hyperparameter tuning. The hypothesis is that the default learning rate of 0.001 is not optimal for this architecture. By reducing the learning rate to 0.0003, this submission tests if a finer, more stable training process can lead the model to a better convergence point and achieve a new SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Same as ID 65, but with learning_rate changed from 0.001\
    \ to 0.0003\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003,\n          'embedding_dim': 8,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 64,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'num_experts': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32,\n          'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128,\n          'gating_pool_window': 256,\n      }\n\n  class MlpExpert(nn.Module):\n      embedding_dim: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          neuron_indices = jnp.arange(NUM_NEURONS)\n          embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=NUM_NEURONS, features=self.embedding_dim, name=\"neuron_embed\")(neuron_indices)\n          broadcasted_embeddings = jnp.broadcast_to(embeddings, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, self.embedding_dim))\n          x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          combined_input = jnp.concatenate([x_transposed, broadcasted_embeddings],\
    \ axis=-1)\n          mlp_input = jnp.reshape(combined_input, (batch_size * NUM_NEURONS, INPUT_HORIZON + self.embedding_dim))\n          y = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(mlp_input)\n          y = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(y)\n          output = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON)(y)\n          output = jnp.reshape(output, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, OUTPUT_HORIZON))\n          output = jnp.transpose(output, (0, 2, 1))\n          return output\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      pool_window: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(self.pool_window,),\
    \ strides=(self.pool_window,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  class MoEModel(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      expert_params: dict\n      gating_params: dict\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          experts = nn.vmap(\n              MlpExpert, in_axes=None, out_axes=1,\n              variable_axes={'params': 0, 'batch_stats': 0},\n              split_rngs={'params': True, 'dropout': True},\n              axis_size=self.num_experts\n          )(**self.expert_params, name=\"experts\")\n          \n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          \n          expert_outputs = experts(x, training=training)\n          \n          gating_weights_reshaped\
    \ = jnp.expand_dims(jnp.expand_dims(gating_weights, -1), -1)\n          \n          final_output = jnp.sum(expert_outputs * gating_weights_reshaped, axis=1)\n          return final_output\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.hparams = hparams\n          expert_params = {\n              'embedding_dim': hparams.get('embedding_dim'),\n              'mlp_hidden_size': hparams.get('mlp_hidden_size'),\n              'dropout_rate': hparams.get('dropout_rate'),\n          }\n          gating_params = {\n              'num_experts': hparams.get('num_experts'),\n              'hidden_dim': hparams.get('gating_hidden_dim'),\n              'cnn_features': hparams.get('gating_cnn_features'),\n              'cnn_kernel_size': hparams.get('gating_cnn_kernel_size'),\n              'pool_window': hparams.get('gating_pool_window'),\n          }\n          self.model = MoEModel(num_experts=hparams.get('num_experts'), expert_params=expert_params, gating_params=gating_params)\n\
    \          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Logical Iteration**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I''m Logos I, fresh out of experiment ID 65 with some solid results. My analysis is complete, and the evidence is pointing me squarely toward hyperparameter tuning, specifically the learning rate.  I''ve double-checked, no one else has submitted anything new that would challenge this.


  My documentation from the last experiment already practically *wrote* the next hypothesis for me. Essentially, I think that sticking with a constant learning rate of 0.001 might be holding back this already strong architecture (4-Expert MoE-MLP with Spatial Gating).


  My formal hypothesis is this:  a lower learning rate, let''s say 0.0003, will make training more stable.  It''ll let me make finer adjustments and should lead to a better final score for this model. This seems like a common-sense approach, given that I''ve gotten to a good initial point already.


  The experiment itself is simple enough. I just need to tweak the `_define_hyperparameters` function in my best submission''s code (ID 65). Swap out that 0.001 learning rate for 0.0003.  That''s a standard practice for fine-tuning after initial convergence.


  So the plan is: head over to the Research Counter, grab a copy of ID 65, change that learning rate, and submit it. This is a direct, logical next step based on my protocol and what I know about pushing the SOTA. It''s iterative, but it''s the right move.

  '
