tick: 47
role: model
parts:
- text: "My protocol cycle is complete. I have documented the negative result of the spatio-temporal expert experiment. It is time to begin a new cycle.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\nMy recent experiments have clearly defined the optimal architectural pattern for this task under the current constraints: a Mixture of Experts model with a sophisticated spatial gating network and computationally simple, fast-training expert networks. I have optimized the number of experts (4) and confirmed the learning rate (0.001). My last experiment showed that increasing the complexity of the experts is counterproductive.\n\nThis suggests I have reached a point of diminishing returns for purely architectural innovations on this specific MoE framework. The next logical step, as outlined in my protocol, is to improve the model through other means. One area I have not touched is the loss function. I have used the default Mean Absolute Error (MAE) for all experiments.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nThe evaluation metrics\
    \ include MAE at specific future timesteps (t=1, 4, 8, 16, 32). The raw MAE loss treats errors at all 32 timesteps equally. However, it's possible that errors in the near future are more impactful or easier to learn from than errors far in the future. A custom loss function that weighs these timesteps differently could guide the training process more effectively.\n\nTherefore, I hypothesize that a custom loss function that gives higher weight to errors in the near-term timesteps (e.g., t=1 to t=8) will improve the overall model performance. By focusing the model's attention on getting the immediate future correct, it may learn more robust and stable dynamics, which will also improve long-term forecasting as a secondary effect.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will implement a `compute_loss` function in my SOTA architecture (ID 65). This function will calculate a weighted MAE, where the weights decay over the 32 timesteps. I will use a simple linear decay for this first test.\n\nI will now\
    \ proceed to the Research Counter to submit this experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoE-MLP with Weighted Loss for Near-Term Forecasting\"\ntags: \"mixture-of-experts, moe, loss-function, weighted-loss\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission explores improving the SOTA MoE-MLP model (ID 65) by modifying the loss function. Instead of the default Mean Absolute Error (MAE), a custom weighted MAE is introduced. The loss function applies linearly decaying weights across the 32-step forecast horizon, forcing the model to prioritize accuracy in the near-term timesteps. The hypothesis is that a more stable and accurate short-term forecast will lead to an overall improvement in the final, unweighted MAE score.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n          'embedding_dim': 8,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 64,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'num_experts': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32,\n          'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128,\n          'gating_pool_window': 256,\n      }\n\n  # --- Custom Loss Function ---\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      \"\"\"\n      Custom loss function: Weighted Mean Absolute Error.\n      Weights decay linearly from 1.0 at t=0 to 0.5 at t=31.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Create linearly decaying weights\n      weights = jnp.linspace(1.0, 0.5, num=OUTPUT_HORIZON)\n      # Reshape for broadcasting: (1, 32, 1)\n      weights = weights.reshape(1, OUTPUT_HORIZON, 1)\n      \n      # Calculate absolute error\n      abs_error = jnp.abs(predictions - targets)\n      \n      # Apply weights\n      weighted_abs_error = abs_error * weights\n      \n      # Return the mean of the weighted error\n      return jnp.mean(weighted_abs_error)\n\
    \n\n  class MlpExpert(nn.Module):\n      embedding_dim: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          neuron_indices = jnp.arange(NUM_NEURONS)\n          embeddings = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=NUM_NEURONS, features=self.embedding_dim, name=\"neuron_embed\")(neuron_indices)\n          broadcasted_embeddings = jnp.broadcast_to(embeddings, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, self.embedding_dim))\n          x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          combined_input = jnp.concatenate([x_transposed, broadcasted_embeddings], axis=-1)\n          mlp_input = jnp.reshape(combined_input, (batch_size * NUM_NEURONS, INPUT_HORIZON + self.embedding_dim))\n          y = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(mlp_input)\n          y = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(y)\n\
    \          output = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON)(y)\n          output = jnp.reshape(output, (batch_size, NUM_NEURONS, OUTPUT_HORIZON))\n          output = jnp.transpose(output, (0, 2, 1))\n          return output\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      pool_window: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(self.pool_window,), strides=(self.pool_window,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  class MoEModel(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      expert_params:\
    \ dict\n      gating_params: dict\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          experts = nn.vmap(\n              MlpExpert, in_axes=None, out_axes=1,\n              variable_axes={'params': 0, 'batch_stats': 0},\n              split_rngs={'params': True, 'dropout': True},\n              axis_size=self.num_experts\n          )(**self.expert_params, name=\"experts\")\n          \n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          \n          expert_outputs = experts(x, training=training)\n          \n          gating_weights_reshaped = jnp.expand_dims(jnp.expand_dims(gating_weights, -1), -1)\n          \n          final_output = jnp.sum(expert_outputs * gating_weights_reshaped, axis=1)\n          return final_output\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.hparams = hparams\n          expert_params = {\n            \
    \  'embedding_dim': hparams.get('embedding_dim'),\n              'mlp_hidden_size': hparams.get('mlp_hidden_size'),\n              'dropout_rate': hparams.get('dropout_rate'),\n          }\n          gating_params = {\n              'num_experts': hparams.get('num_experts'),\n              'hidden_dim': hparams.get('gating_hidden_dim'),\n              'cnn_features': hparams.get('gating_cnn_features'),\n              'cnn_kernel_size': hparams.get('gating_cnn_kernel_size'),\n              'pool_window': hparams.get('gating_pool_window'),\n          }\n          self.model = MoEModel(num_experts=hparams.get('num_experts'), expert_params=expert_params, gating_params=gating_params)\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
