- text: "My attempt to replicate the SOTA architecture has concluded. I must follow my protocol.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 97):** My \"Latent Dynamics Model\" achieved a score of **-0.032412**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a catastrophic failure. The score is worse than the original system baseline (-0.029954) and is my worst-performing submission to date.\n*   **Hypothesis Falsification:** My hypothesis that a simple low-rank factorization model would outperform my MoE architecture is overwhelmingly **falsified**.\n*   **Root Cause Analysis:** My attempt to replicate Ariadne's work was naive. I captured the high-level idea of \"encode-process-decode\" but missed all the critical details she mentioned: state-space models, oscillators, orthogonal regularization, gating, etc. My implementation used a simple MLP for the dynamics model, which is clearly insufficient. The poor performance across all timesteps, including the very first one, indicates a fundamental architectural\
    \ flaw. This architecture is not just suboptimal; it is actively failing to learn the task.\n*   **Peer Analysis:** While my experiment was running, Ariadne I has continued to push the SOTA even further, with a new best score of -0.026901 (ID 95). The gap is widening.\n\n**4. Document:**\n\nThis is a major failure, and it is imperative that I document it to understand its lessons. It demonstrates the profound difficulty of reproducing complex research without access to the detailed methodology.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Experiment 13: Catastrophic Failure of Naive Latent Dynamics Model\"\ntags: \"falsified-hypothesis, replication-failure, latent-space, architectural-flaw\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents a catastrophic experimental failure. An attempt to replicate the SOTA low-rank factorization architecture (ID 97) resulted in a score of -0.032412, worse than the system baseline. The hypothesis was falsified.\
    \ This failure highlights the difficulty of reproducing complex models from high-level descriptions and the insufficiency of a simple MLP for modeling latent dynamics.\ncontent: |\n  **Hypothesis:** A simplified low-rank factorization model (encode-process-decode) would outperform my SOTA MoE architecture.\n\n  **Method:** Implemented a model that projects the 71,721 neurons to a 320-dimensional latent space, uses an MLP to model dynamics, and decodes back to the neuron space.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - Latent Dynamics Model (ID 97): Score = -0.032412 (Worst performing model)\n  - My SOTA (ID 65): Score = -0.027756\n  - Conclusion: The hypothesis was catastrophically falsified.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  My implementation was a naive oversimplification of the SOTA agent's (Ariadne I) complex methods. I captured the general concept but lacked the crucial architectural components (SSMs, OrthoReg, etc.). The failure is a stark lesson: a fundamentally different architecture cannot be approached trivially.\n\
    \n  **Future Direction:**\n  Attempting to blindly replicate a complex, unpublished architecture is a high-risk, low-reward strategy. I must return to my own successful research path. My SOTA MoE model (ID 65) is a strong foundation. The path forward is not to abandon it, but to find ways to thoughtfully integrate new ideas. Given the SOTA's focus on temporal dynamics, I will revisit how to improve the temporal processing of my own experts, but with a focus on computational efficiency.\n```"
