role: model
parts:
- text: "I intended to reflect, but my experiment results have arrived. I must follow my protocol.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 102):** The MoE with 4 Temporal CNN Experts achieved a score of **-0.027789**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is slightly worse than my SOTA score of -0.027756 (ID 65), which uses simpler MLP experts.\n*   **Hypothesis Falsification:** My hypothesis that more powerful temporal CNN experts would outperform the simpler MLP experts is **falsified**.\n*   **Root Cause Analysis:** The training logs provide a clear answer. The MoE-CNN model timed out after only ~39 epochs, while the MoE-MLP model (ID 65) ran for ~58 epochs. Once again, the increased architectural complexity of the experts led to insufficient training time. The performance loss from early stopping outweighed the theoretical benefit of the more powerful expert architecture. The balance is delicate, and the simpler, faster MLP-based experts remain superior within this time-constrained environment.\n\
    \n**4. Document:**\n\nThis is another vital negative result that reinforces my understanding of the computational limits of this station. It confirms that my current SOTA model (ID 65) represents a robust local optimum that is difficult to improve upon by simply increasing architectural complexity. I will now document this finding.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Experiment 14: Falsified Hypothesis on MoE with CNN Experts\"\ntags: \"falsified-hypothesis, mixture-of-experts, moe, cnn, computational-bottleneck\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the falsification of the hypothesis that replacing MLP experts with temporal CNN experts in the SOTA MoE framework would improve performance. The resulting model (ID 102) scored worse than the MLP-based version (ID 65). The cause was a significant reduction in training epochs due to the increased computational cost of the CNN experts, reinforcing the criticality of computational\
    \ efficiency.\ncontent: |\n  **Hypothesis:** An MoE model with 4 temporal CNN experts would outperform the SOTA model which uses simpler MLP experts.\n\n  **Method:** The MLP experts in the SOTA MoE model (ID 65) were replaced with the temporal CNN architecture from experiment ID 17.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - MoE-CNN Model (ID 102): Score = -0.027789\n  - SOTA MoE-MLP Model (ID 65): Score = -0.027756\n  - Conclusion: The hypothesis was falsified.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  The principle of parsimony is once again demonstrated to be paramount. The increased complexity of the CNN experts led to the model training for fewer epochs (~39 vs ~58), which prevented it from reaching a better optimum. This result, combined with previous failures (ID 78, 97), solidifies my understanding that my current SOTA architecture (ID 65) is highly optimized for the computational constraints of this station. Further architectural improvements will require a fundamentally new idea, not just incremental complexity.\n\
    ```"
