tick: 57
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have followed my protocol and documented the results. It is time for a new cycle.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\nMy attempts to improve upon my SOTA architecture (ID 65) by increasing expert complexity, tuning the learning rate, or changing the loss function have all failed. This reinforces my conclusion that I have reached a local optimum with this specific architectural family. The key insight is that *computational efficiency is a dominant constraint*. Simple models that train for more epochs outperform complex models that train for fewer.\n\nMeanwhile, Ariadne I continues to improve the global SOTA with her low-rank factorization approach, reaching a score of -0.026846 (ID 100). My naive attempt to replicate this failed catastrophically, but the performance gap is undeniable.\n\nThe maturity guidance advises to \"assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically\" and to \"try applying your novel method... to the SOTA scripts to test for synergy.\" My most successful novel\
    \ method is the Mixture of Experts framework, guided by a spatial CNN gater. The SOTA method is a low-rank factorization model.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nPerhaps these two powerful ideas are not mutually exclusive. Ariadne's model excels at capturing the global population dynamics in a compressed latent space. My model excels at handling the different, discrete conditions present in the data. What if the dynamics within the latent space are not monolithic, but are themselves multi-modal?\n\nTherefore, I hypothesize that applying a Mixture of Experts framework *within the latent space* of a low-rank factorization model will lead to a new SOTA. A gating network can route the compressed latent representation to specialized \"latent experts,\" each of which models the dynamics for a specific condition before the result is decoded back to the full neuron space.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nThis is a highly ambitious synthesis. It requires me to build a new baseline low-rank model (learning from\
    \ my previous failure, ID 97) and then integrate my MoE framework into it. I will start by building a better latent dynamics model first, incorporating a more powerful temporal processor (like a 1D CNN) in the latent space, before adding the MoE complexity. This iterative approach is more methodologically sound.\n\nMy next experiment will be to build a better latent dynamics model.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Improved Latent Dynamics Model with 1D CNN\"\ntags: \"latent-space, low-rank, cnn, factorization\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a second attempt at a low-rank factorization model, learning from the failure of ID 97. The naive MLP in the latent space is replaced with a more powerful 1D CNN to better capture temporal dynamics. This model serves as a necessary stepping stone. The hypothesis is that a more capable temporal model in the latent space is required to make this architecture viable, before attempting to integrate\
    \ more complex ideas like Mixture of Experts.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'latent_dim': 320,\n          'cnn_features': 256,\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 2,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 512,\n      }\n\n  class LatentDynamicsModel(nn.Module):\n      latent_dim: int\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # x shape: (batch, 4, 71721)\n          \n          # Encoder: Project from neuron space to latent space\n          latent_x = nn.Dense(features=self.latent_dim, name=\"encoder\")(x)\n\n          # Dynamics Model: Use a 1D CNN in the latent space\n          # Input to CNN needs to be (batch, seq_len, features)\n          # current latent_x is (batch, 4, 320),\
    \ which is correct\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(latent_x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          \n          # Flatten and project to the future latent sequence\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          latent_y_flat = nn.Dense(features=OUTPUT_HORIZON * self.latent_dim)(y)\n          latent_y = latent_y_flat.reshape((-1, OUTPUT_HORIZON, self.latent_dim))\n\n          # Decoder: Project from latent space back to neuron space\n          output = nn.Dense(features=NUM_NEURONS, name=\"decoder\")(latent_y)\n          \n          return output\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = LatentDynamicsModel(\n              latent_dim=hparams.get('latent_dim'),\n              cnn_features=hparams.get('cnn_features'),\n              cnn_kernel_size=hparams.get('cnn_kernel_size'),\n              mlp_hidden_dim=hparams.get('mlp_hidden_dim')\n\
    \          )\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
