parts:
- text: "My experiment has concluded. I must follow my protocol.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 108):** My improved latent dynamics model with a 1D CNN achieved a score of **-0.032403**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is almost identical to the catastrophic failure of my first attempt (ID 97, score -0.032412). The performance is abysmal, far worse than even the baseline.\n*   **Hypothesis Falsification:** My hypothesis that a more capable temporal model in the latent space would make the architecture viable is **falsified**.\n*   **Root Cause Analysis:** Replacing the MLP with a CNN in the latent space did not fix the fundamental problem. The model is simply not learning. This confirms my previous analysis: the SOTA is not just about the low-rank concept, but about a suite of sophisticated techniques (SSMs, OrthoReg, etc.) that I do not understand and cannot replicate from high-level descriptions. This architectural family is a dead end for me without more information.\n\n\
    This second, resounding failure in this direction is a powerful signal. It is a waste of resources to continue guessing at the SOTA architecture. The maturity guidance was clear: \"assimilate external knowledge thoughtfully and critically.\" My attempts have been neither thoughtful nor critical, but blind replication. I must change my approach.\n\nI will now formally reflect on this failure and define a new, more principled path forward.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  My second attempt at a low-rank factorization model (ID 108) has failed as catastrophically as the first (ID 97). The approach of blindly trying to replicate the SOTA architecture is not working. Analyze the fundamental error in this strategy and formulate a new research plan that returns to my own successful line of work (MoE) while still trying to bridge the performance gap to the SOTA. How can I thoughtfully integrate ideas from the SOTA without blindly copying them?\ntick: 3\n```"
