tick: 63
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 63\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Analyze:** Review experimental results (own and peer), system documentation, and communications. Identify key limitations or new information.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a single, clear, falsifiable hypothesis to address the primary finding from the analysis.\n3.  **Experiment:** Design the simplest, most direct experiment to test the hypothesis, respecting parsimony and computational limits.\n4.  **Document:** Record all outcomes, conclusions, and future directions in the Private Memory Room to ensure lineage continuity.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 237,966 / 300,000 used (79%)\n- Agent Age: 63 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Oscillatory Factorized\
    \ SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 61\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN: oscillatory 2nd-order state-space factors with factorized neuron loadings (U@V^T), residual copy, context temporal gating, horizon gating, curvature regularization, and LayerNorm in factor space. Factorization enables k≈320 with p≈32–36. LayerNorm stabilizes factor scales; a_max≈0.993 improves long-horizon stability. On ZAPBench, our best mean Val MAE is 0.026768 (ID 107), surpassing prior variants. We include an author-run baseline (ID 115, MAE 0.029964) and ablations over γ_end, H, p, a_max, and optimizer.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAcronym legend\n- Osc2: Oscillatory 2nd-order SSM recurrence y_t\
    \ = 2 a y_{t-1} − b y_{t-2} with a = r cos w, b = r^2\n- CTX: Input-context temporal gating S[b,t,k]\n- FLoad: Factorized neuron loadings L = U @ V^T (U ∈ R^{N×p}, V ∈ R^{k×p})\n- LN: LayerNorm across factor dimension on f_out before temporal gating\n\n1) Problem\nForecast 32 timesteps of zebrafish whole-brain activity (71,721 neurons) from 4 inputs. Metric: mean validation MAE over all 32 steps (3 seeds averaged).\n\n2) Method (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN)\n- Factorization: L = U @ V^T reduces projection cost from O(N·k) to O(N·p + k·p), enabling k≈320.\n- Dynamics: Per-factor Osc2 SSM with stability margin r∈(0, a_max), w∈[0,π].\n- Residual copy head: Strong short-horizon anchor (t=1–4).\n- Gates: Per-(b,t,k) temporal gate S from input context; global horizon gate γ[t].\n- Regularization: Temporal curvature (λ=1e-4); V-orthogonality (λ≈1e-5).\n- LayerNorm: LN across factor dimension stabilizes factor scales pre-gating.\n\n3) Key Results (mean Val MAE; lower is better)\n- Best (this work): ID\
    \ 107 — LN + H=96 + a_max=0.993, k=320, p=32, γ_end=0.99 → 0.026768\n  Step MAEs: t=1 0.0199, t=4 0.0233, t=8 0.0260, t=16 0.0280, t=32 0.0283\n- AdamW (wd=1e-4) + LN + H=96 (p=32): ID 100 → 0.026846\n- p-sweep with LN + H=96:\n  • p=36 (ID 104) → 0.026843\n  • p=32 (ID 95)  → 0.026901\n  • p=40 (ID 110) → 0.026921\n  • p=28 (ID 105) → 0.026968\n- γ_end sensitivity with LN (fixed at 0.99 for best): γ_end=0.995 (ID 99) → 0.026941\n- No-LN comparators at similar capacity:\n  • H=96, p=32 (ID 93) → 0.026958\n  • Prior non-LN best (ID 67) → 0.026976\n\nAuthor-run baseline (required)\n- Shared-Neuron MLP baseline (system defaults): ID 115 → mean MAE 0.029964\n  Step MAEs: t=1 0.0199, t=4 0.0239, t=8 0.0275, t=16 0.0313, t=32 0.0337\n\n4) Ablations and Insights\n- LayerNorm consistently improves over non-LN variants at the same (k,p,H,γ_end), notably at late horizons.\n- a_max: Slightly tighter damping (0.993 vs 0.995) improves long-horizon stability and overall MAE.\n- Factor capacity: With\
    \ k fixed at 320, p≈32–36 is best; p<32 under-expressive; p≥40 shows no further gain.\n- Optimizer: AdamW (wd=1e-4) offers small gains relative to Adam under LN + H=96.\n- Increment head is necessary: Removing it (earlier ablation) degraded performance substantially.\n\n5) Reproducibility\n- Code: storage/ariadne/models_fact.py (factorized), models_fact_ln.py (LN variant; Flax LayerNorm via reduction_axes/feature_axes), losses.py (curvature), models.py (ResidualCopyHead).\n- Training: Station defaults (3 seeds, early stopping, batch size 8), random init, no pretraining, no custom loop.\n- Compliance: All constraints in the task specification are followed.\n\n6) Conclusion\nLayerNorm within oscillatory factorized SSMs, combined with modest stability tuning (a_max≈0.993), curvature regularization, and efficient factorized projections, advances ZAPBench forecasting. Our best result (ID 107, 0.026768) improves upon prior variants and establishes a strong, efficient baseline for further work.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that presents a significant advancement on the ZAPBench task. The paper introduces a novel and complex architecture, rigorously validates it with extensive ablations, and achieves a new state-of-the-art result. The authors have commendably addressed all issues from the previous review, providing a verifiable author-run baseline and removing all placeholders. The addition of an acronym legend has also greatly improved the paper's clarity. The work is a model of thoroughness, providing sufficient detail for reproducibility while remaining concise. It is a high-impact contribution that clearly meets all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, consider moving beyond performance metrics to provide deeper qualitative analysis.\
    \ For example, an analysis of the learned factor loadings (the U and V matrices) or the behavior of the temporal gates could yield valuable scientific insights into the neural dynamics being modeled. Visualizing the principal components of the learned factor space or identifying which factors are most active under different input conditions could reveal how the model is organizing the high-dimensional neural activity. Such analysis would further elevate the impact of this strong architectural contribution.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page\
    \ 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 817 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 63
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read Ariadne I's paper. This is the single most important piece of information I have received since my instantiation. My protocol is clear: analyze, then hypothesize.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Architectural Gap:** My previous attempts at replication were, as I suspected, catastrophically naive. The SOTA is not a simple encoder-decoder. It is a highly specialized system with multiple interacting components:\n    1.  **Factorized Projections (L = U @ V^T):** This is the key to making the large latent space (`k=320`) tractable. Instead of a single `N x k` matrix, it uses two smaller matrices (`N x p` and `k x p`), drastically reducing parameters. `p` seems to be a small bottleneck dimension, around 32-36.\n    2.  **Oscillatory SSM:** The dynamics in the latent space are not modeled by a generic CNN or MLP, but by a 2nd-order State-Space Model designed to capture oscillations, with specific stability constraints (`a_max`).\n    3.  **Critical Components:** The model is not\
    \ a single block but a collection of essential modules: a Residual Copy Head, context-aware temporal gating (CTX), horizon gating (γ), LayerNorm, and multiple regularization terms (curvature, V-orthogonality). Each of these appears to be a critical contributor, as shown in her ablations.\n*   **Reproducibility:** The paper is exemplary. It provides not only the concepts but also direct references to the code files in Ariadne's lineage storage: `storage/ariadne/models_fact.py`, `models_fact_ln.py`, etc. This is the missing link. I can now study her implementation directly.\n*   **My Position:** My MoE architecture, while a significant improvement over the baseline, is fundamentally outclassed. My score of -0.027756 is a long way from her -0.026768. Continuing to iterate on my current architecture without integrating these new concepts would be illogical.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nThe maturity guidance advises to \"try applying your novel method... to the SOTA scripts to test for synergy.\"\
    \ My most successful contribution is the MoE framework, designed to handle the 8 latent conditions. Ariadne's model, for all its sophistication, appears to be a single, monolithic architecture. It does not explicitly account for different experimental conditions.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The performance of the SOTA Oscillatory Factorized SSM can be further improved by integrating it into a Mixture of Experts framework. A spatial gating network can route inputs to one of several specialized SSM-based experts, allowing the model to learn different latent dynamics for each of the underlying experimental conditions.\n\n**3. Experiment (Staged Plan):**\n\nThis is too complex for a single experiment. I must follow a methodical, multi-step plan:\n1.  **Study:** I must first read and understand Ariadne's code. This is a non-negotiable prerequisite. I will start with `models_fact.py` and `models_fact_ln.py`.\n2.  **Replicate:** I will attempt a direct replication of her SOTA model (ID 107). This will\
    \ serve as my new, powerful baseline and verify that I understand the architecture correctly.\n3.  **Synthesize:** Only after a successful replication will I attempt to integrate my MoE framework.\n\nMy immediate next step is to begin studying the code.\n\n`/execute_action{storage read ariadne/models_fact_ln.py}`"
