tick: 85
role: model
parts:
- text: "My replication of Episteme's SOTA has concluded. I am in the Reflection Chamber.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 157):** My replication scored **-0.026931**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is an excellent result. It is very close to Episteme's original score of -0.026743, with the difference easily attributable to stochasticity.\n*   **Hypothesis Validation:** My hypothesis that I could correctly implement the new SOTA paradigm is **validated**. The \"Radical Parsimony\" pivot was a success. I have corrected my flawed path of over-engineering and now possess a simple, powerful, and well-understood baseline that is competitive with the station's best.\n*   **Path Forward:** I am now on solid ground. I have a SOTA-level model that I built and understand. My original research strength was the MoE framework. Now that I have a *computationally efficient* expert, my original synthesis hypothesis is viable once more.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nI will now formally re-state my\
    \ primary synthesis hypothesis, but this time with a validated, efficient expert:\n\nA Mixture of Experts model, where each expert is an instance of the parsimonious \"Factorized MLP + RC + LN\" architecture, will outperform the single-expert SOTA. The spatial gating network will route inputs to the most appropriate parsimonious expert, combining the benefits of both approaches and achieving a new global SOTA.\n\nThis is the same hypothesis as ID 144, but this time it is computationally feasible.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will now re-run the experiment from ID 144, but with my new, correct, and self-implemented parsimonious expert. This is the logical culmination of my entire research journey, combining my MoE innovation with the station's SOTA architecture in a computationally viable way.\n\nI will first document this successful replication, then submit the final synthesis experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I -\
    \ Experiment 19: Successful Replication of Episteme's Parsimonious SOTA\"\ntags: \"replication, success, parsimony, factorized, sota, baseline\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the successful replication of Episteme I's parsimonious SOTA (ID 157). By re-implementing his \"Factorized MLP + RC + LN\" model based on his paper, I achieved a score of -0.026931, validating the \"Radical Parsimony\" pivot and establishing a powerful, efficient, and well-understood baseline for my lineage.\ncontent: |\n  **Hypothesis:** I can successfully replicate Episteme I's SOTA model (ID 129) by re-implementing it based on his published paper.\n\n  **Method:** A new model was implemented from scratch, combining Factorized Loadings, a Residual Copy Head, and LayerNorm with an MLP forecaster, as described in Archive #2.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - My Replication (ID 157): Score = -0.026931\n  - Episteme's SOTA (ID 129): Score = -0.026743\n  - Conclusion: The hypothesis was validated. The replication was\
    \ highly successful.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  After a series of failures with over-engineered models, this success proves that a return to first principles and parsimony is the correct path. I now have a SOTA-competitive model that I understand completely.\n\n  **Future Direction:**\n  The path is now clear to test my grand synthesis hypothesis: combining this new, computationally efficient expert with my proven Mixture of Experts framework.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Synthesis: MoE with Parsimonious SOTA Experts\"\ntags: \"mixture-of-experts, moe, factorized, parsimony, synthesis, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the definitive test of my grand synthesis hypothesis. It combines my successful MoE framework with my validated, re-implemented version of Episteme I's parsimonious SOTA architecture. The model uses a spatial CNN gater to route inputs to one of four \"Factorized MLP + RC + LN\" experts. Unlike previous failed attempts,\
    \ this synthesis is built on a computationally efficient expert, making it a viable test of whether specializing parsimonious SOTA experts can achieve a new global SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  class FactorizedMlpRcExpert(nn.Module):\n      latent_dim: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (NUM_NEURONS, self.proj_rank))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.latent_dim, self.proj_rank))\n          \n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,kp->btk', factors_in, V)\n\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n  \
    \        \n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_hidden_dim)(factors_flat)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(features=OUTPUT_HORIZON * self.latent_dim)(y)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, self.latent_dim))\n\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(reduction_axes=-1, feature_axes=-1)(factors_out)\n          \n          y_factor_p = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', y_factor_p, U)\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n          \n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      pool_window: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n\
    \          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(self.pool_window,), strides=(self.pool_window,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  class MoE_Final_Synthesis(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      expert_params: dict\n      gating_params: dict\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          experts = nn.vmap(\n              FactorizedMlpRcExpert, in_axes=None, out_axes=1,\n              variable_axes={'params': 0},\n              split_rngs={'params': True},\n              axis_size=self.num_experts\n          )(**self.expert_params, name=\"experts\")\n          \n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n          expert_outputs = experts(x,\
    \ training=training)\n          \n          gating_weights_reshaped = jnp.expand_dims(jnp.expand_dims(gating_weights, -1), -1)\n          final_output = jnp.sum(expert_outputs * gating_weights_reshaped, axis=1)\n          return final_output\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          # Expert params from Episteme's SOTA\n          'latent_dim': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 512,\n          # My proven Gating params\n          'num_experts': 4,\n          'gating_hidden_dim': 32,\n          'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n          'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128,\n          'gating_pool_window': 256,\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          expert_params = {k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in ['latent_dim', 'proj_rank', 'mlp_hidden_dim']}\n          gating_params = {k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in ['num_experts', 'hidden_dim', 'cnn_features', 'cnn_kernel_size',\
    \ 'pool_window']}\n          gating_params['hidden_dim'] = hparams.get('gating_hidden_dim') # manual fix for name mismatch\n          self.model = MoE_Final_Synthesis(num_experts=hparams.get('num_experts'), expert_params=expert_params, gating_params=gating_params)\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
