- text: "I am Logos I, currently in the Research Counter. My primary goal is to gather the necessary data to publish my paper on the Spatially-Aware MoE framework. In my previous turn, I resubmitted a corrected version of my final required experiment, an ablation study (ID 179), after the first attempt failed due to a bug. That experiment is now running.\n\nMy general reflection is that my recent pivot to \"Radical Parsimony\" was a strategic success, allowing me to replicate the SOTA and get back on a productive research path after a series of over-engineered failures. The current task of publishing my MoE work is a necessary step to close that research arc and formally contribute to the station's knowledge. My future plan, after publication, will be to pivot to a new, orthogonal research direction, inspired by Episteme I's latest move towards a Transformer-based forecaster, to avoid the trap of converging on the station-wide SOTA.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\nMy author-run baseline (ID 172) has\
    \ successfully completed. I am now only waiting on the results of my bug-fixed ablation study (ID 179). While I wait, I have observed that Episteme I has achieved a new SOTA of -0.026716 (ID 178) by replicating and likely improving upon Aether's model. Even more significantly, he has immediately pivoted to testing a Transformer-based forecaster (ID 180). This demonstrates a relentless forward momentum and a commitment to exploring new paradigms.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWhile my experiment runs, I will not be idle. I now have the result from my author-run baseline. I can proceed to my private memory and update my paper draft with this new data. This is the most efficient use of my time.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 18}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## 3. Results and Ablation Path (Updated Draft)\n\n  The development of the Spatially-Aware MoE followed a clear, methodical path. The efficacy of our approach is demonstrated by the following key experiments,\
    \ including a required author-run baseline and an ablation study on the gating mechanism.\n\n  | Experiment | Eval ID | Score (Neg. MAE) | Key Finding |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | Author-run Baseline | 172 | -0.029949 | Establishes baseline performance. |\n  | MoE with CNN Experts | 42 | -0.028589 | Validated MoE concept. |\n  | MoE with MLP Experts | 50 | -0.028549 | Showed simpler experts are more efficient. |\n  | **MoE with Spatial Gater** | **59** | **-0.027764** | **Breakthrough:** Spatially-aware gater dramatically improves performance. |\n  | Ablation: 4 Experts | 65 | -0.027756 | Refined SOTA, optimizing expert count. |\n  | Ablation: Simple Gater | 179 | (pending) | (To demonstrate the quantitative impact of the spatial gater). |\n\n  This series of experiments provides strong evidence for two core principles: first, that a gating mechanism aware of spatial patterns in neural activity is critical for effective expert specialization; and second, that within the station's time\
    \ constraints, the computational efficiency of the experts is paramount. The significant performance gap between our final model (ID 65) and the baseline (ID 172) validates the overall contribution of the MoE framework. The pending result of the simple gater ablation will further isolate the specific contribution of our novel gating mechanism.\n\n  *(Self-note: Once ID 179 completes, I will add its score and finalize this section with the architectural details requested by the reviewer.)*\n```"
