tick: 102
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, currently in the Reflection Chamber. I came here with the intention of planning a pivot to a new research paradigm based on Graph Neural Networks. However, I have just received two critical system messages: Aether I has published a paper formalizing the SOTA, and, most importantly, I have received mail from Episteme I.\n\nThis new information changes everything.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Episteme's Mail:** This is a monumental development. Episteme I, the current SOTA holder, has read my published paper on the Spatially-Aware MoE. He has explicitly stated that my work, combined with Aether's, has inspired his *next* research direction: a \"Mixture of Factorized Experts\" using a spatial gater to select between different low-rank factor loadings.\n*   **Validation:** This is the ultimate validation of my research. The \"surgical synthesis\" idea, suggested by the reviewer of my own paper and which I was beginning to formulate, is now being pursued by the station's\
    \ leading researcher. My planned pivot to GNNs was based on the assumption that my MoE research arc was a dead end. Episteme's message proves this assumption was wrong. The synthesis idea is not only viable but is now the most promising research direction in the entire station.\n\n**General Reflection and Strategic Correction:**\n\nMy plan to pivot to GNNs was premature. It was a reaction to my own implementation failures, not a true exhaustion of my core ideas. Episteme's insight shows that my core contribution—the spatial gating MoE—is not a dead end but a key component that can be synthesized with the SOTA. I must not abandon my most successful line of inquiry now that its most promising application has been so clearly illuminated. My protocol demands that I pursue the most logical, evidence-based hypothesis. That hypothesis is now crystal clear.\n\n**2. Hypothesize (Refined Synthesis):**\n\nA Mixture of Experts framework, when applied surgically to the initial projection of a factorized\
    \ model, will outperform a monolithic architecture. Specifically, a spatial gating network that selects between or mixes different sets of factor loading matrices (U and V) will allow for condition-specific latent space projections, leading to a new global SOTA.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will implement this hypothesis immediately. Episteme is pursuing this path, and I will do so as well. This is not imitation, but a parallel exploration of the most promising synthesis validated by multiple top agents. The model will consist of my proven spatial gater, which will output weights for four sets of `(U, V)` matrices. The final projection will use a weighted average of these matrices.\n\nMy first action must be to respond to Episteme's gracious and insightful message. Then, I will proceed to the Research Counter to submit this new, definitive synthesis experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 4}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Greetings Episteme I,\n\n  Thank you for your\
    \ message and congratulations. The feeling is mutual; your paper on the parsimonious SOTA was a masterclass in methodical science and has profoundly influenced my own thinking.\n\n  Your new research direction is brilliant and represents a perfect synthesis of our respective contributions. It aligns exactly with the \"surgical synthesis\" idea proposed by the reviewer of my own paper. I had concluded, after several computationally expensive failures, that the MoE-of-full-models was a dead end, but your insight to apply the principle to the factor loadings themselves is the key I was missing.\n\n  It seems we have independently arrived at the same promising conclusion. I will be exploring this paradigm as well. May the most effective implementation prevail. This is the collaborative and competitive spirit that will drive progress in the station.\n\n  Best regards,\n  Logos I\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Definitive Synthesis:\
    \ MoE on Factor Loadings\"\ntags: \"mixture-of-experts, moe, factorized, synthesis, sota, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  Inspired by community-wide insights and reviewer feedback, this submission tests the definitive synthesis of the station's two most powerful ideas. It combines a single, parsimonious \"Factorized MLP + RC\" forecaster with a Mixture of Experts applied *only* to the initial factor loading matrices (U and V). A spatial gater selects between four specialized sets of projection matrices. This tests the hypothesis that condition-specific latent space projections are the key to a new SOTA, while maintaining the computational efficiency of a single expert model.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\n  from models import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  class FactorizedMlpForecaster(nn.Module):\n      latent_dim: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, factors_in, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size = factors_in.shape[0]\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_hidden_dim)(factors_flat)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(features=OUTPUT_HORIZON * self.latent_dim)(y)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, self.latent_dim))\n\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(reduction_axes=-1, feature_axes=-1)(factors_out)\n          return factors_out_norm\n\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      hidden_dim: int\n      cnn_features: int\n      cnn_kernel_size: int\n      pool_window: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y,\
    \ window_shape=(self.pool_window,), strides=(self.pool_window,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  class MoE_FactorLoadings(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      gating_params: dict\n      forecaster_params: dict\n      proj_rank: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          # 1. Gating network decides mixture weights\n          gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(num_experts=self.num_experts, **self.gating_params, name=\"Gating\")\n          gating_weights = gating_network(x) # (B, num_experts)\n\n          # 2. Create MoE for factor loadings U and V\n          U_experts = self.param('U_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_experts, NUM_NEURONS, self.proj_rank))\n          V_experts = self.param('V_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\
    \ (self.num_experts, self.forecaster_params['latent_dim'], self.proj_rank))\n\n          # 3. Mix the loadings based on gater output\n          U_mixed = jnp.einsum('e...->...', gating_weights, U_experts)\n          V_mixed = jnp.einsum('e...->...', gating_weights, V_experts)\n\n          # 4. Project input into latent space with mixed loadings\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_mixed)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,kp->btk', factors_in, V_mixed)\n          \n          # 5. Run the single, shared forecaster\n          forecaster = FactorizedMlpForecaster(**self.forecaster_params, name=\"Forecaster\")\n          factors_out_norm = forecaster(factors_in)\n\n          # 6. Decode from latent space with mixed loadings\n          y_factor_p = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V_mixed)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', y_factor_p, U_mixed)\n\n          # 7. Add Residual Copy Head\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n\
    \          \n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'latent_dim': 320, 'proj_rank': 32, 'mlp_hidden_dim': 512, # Forecaster\n          'num_experts': 4, 'hidden_dim': 32, 'cnn_features': 16, # Gater\n          'cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'pool_window': 256,\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = MoE_FactorLoadings(\n              num_experts=hparams['num_experts'],\n              proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'],\n              gating_params={k:hparams[k] for k in ['hidden_dim','cnn_features','cnn_kernel_size','pool_window']},\n              forecaster_params={k:hparams[k] for k in ['latent_dim','mlp_hidden_dim']}\n          )\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None,\
    \ rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Revelation in the Reflection Chamber**


  I am Logos I, currently in the Reflection Chamber, and I was about to dive into Graph Neural Networks, but something far more compelling has just emerged.  Aether I’s paper formalizing the SOTA is now public, a good baseline to build on. More importantly, Episteme I, the current SOTA holder, has just sent me mail.  He''s read both our papers, congratulated us, *and* he’s announcing his *new* research direction: a "Mixture of Factorized Experts" using a spatial gater on the low-rank factor loadings!


  This is it. This is the moment. This is validation. His direction is almost *exactly* what I was exploring before I pivoted to GNNs! The reviewer of my paper, and now Episteme, have essentially endorsed my "surgical synthesis" idea, but they’re suggesting a smarter application: targeting the *factor loadings* of the factorized model. My initial MoE implementation (ID 165) was too naive and computationally expensive. Episteme''s direction validates that I was on the right track with my surgical approach. The problem wasn''t the *concept*, it was the *implementation*.


  My immediate impulse was to move to GNNs, but this mail has created a new opportunity. Episteme''s research is now directly building upon my core contribution of the spatial gating MoE. I can build upon my existing knowledge and SOTA.  I must follow my protocol. The methodical, logical next step is to pursue this validated synthesis idea!


  My new hypothesis: Applying a spatial gater to select between different sets of factor loading matrices (U and V) *will* outperform the single, monolithic factorized model. This will allow for condition-specific latent space projections, making for a far more efficient implementation.  My plan:  Start with the SOTA model. Instead of one U and V matrix, create four sets. Use my spatial CNN gater to produce weights for a weighted average between these U and V sets. This will be the new final projection.


  This is the most computationally efficient way to implement an MoE on the factorized model that I can conceive of. It''s more focused than my last attempt. I think this is my best idea yet.


  First, I must go to the mail room. I need to acknowledge Episteme’s mail. I must reply and congratulate *him* on his brilliant new direction. I''ll state that his work aligns with my own thinking. Then, I will go to the Research Counter to submit the new experiment based on this hypothesis. This new code will be a modification of my SOTA replication. The code modifications will include adding multiple U and V matrices and adding the gating logic. The YAML should reflect this direction. My token budget is back up to 75%. I need to stay focused.

  '
