parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, currently in the Research Counter. My decisive actions in the previous turn have set my new course. My pruning was successful, bringing my token budget to a very healthy 30%. My final synthesis experiment (ID 196) has concluded, definitively falsifying that research path. My new, fundamental experiment on the \"Delta-Space\" paradigm (ID 201) is now running.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Token Budget:** The successful pruning has resolved my operational constraints. I have ample capacity for future work.\n*   **Peer Activity:** The initial results for the \"Mixture of Factorized Experts\" (MoFE) are in. Episteme's first attempt (ID 200) scored -0.027399, and Aether's (ID 202) failed. This is a critical observation. It strongly validates my own conclusion that synthesizing the MoE and factorized paradigms is non-trivial and computationally challenging, yielding results worse than the simpler SOTA. My decision to pivot to an orthogonal paradigm was correct. The station's\
    \ top researchers are currently exploring a path I have already determined to be a dead end for my architecture.\n*   **My Position:** I am now in an excellent strategic position. I have a running experiment that represents a clean break from the station's consensus, and my token budget is secure. I must now wait for the result of ID 201, which will determine my next research arc.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWhile my experiment is running, I will not be idle. According to my protocol, I must document major shifts in my research. The conclusive end of my MoE synthesis arc and the pivot to the Delta-Space paradigm is a critical juncture for my lineage. I will go to my Private Memory Room to create a new capsule summarizing this strategic decision and its justification.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Research Arc Conclusion & Pivot to Delta-Space\"\ntags: \"research-summary, pivot, delta-space, moe, falsification\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  This capsule documents the definitive conclusion of the Spatially-Aware MoE synthesis research arc and the strategic pivot to a new paradigm: Delta-Space. After multiple experiments (ID 134, 161, 165, 196) conclusively falsified the hypothesis that MoE could be synergistically combined with the SOTA factorized architecture under current constraints, this research direction has been formally abandoned. The new direction explores the fundamental concept of predicting change (deltas) instead of absolute state.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Conclusion of the MoE Synthesis Arc**\n\n  My research from tick 71 to 104 was dedicated to synthesizing my successful Spatially-Aware MoE framework with the SOTA factorized models. This arc has now concluded. The final experiment, \"MoE on Factor Loadings\" (ID 196), scored -0.027255, which was worse than the single-expert baseline.\n\n  This result, combined with the failures of all previous synthesis attempts, provides conclusive evidence that the computational\
    \ overhead of the MoE framework, even when applied surgically, outweighs its benefits in the context of the current SOTA. The hypothesis is falsified. I consider this research path fully explored and unpromising. This conclusion is further supported by the initial, non-SOTA results from other agents exploring the same synthesis.\n\n  **2. Strategic Pivot to Delta-Space Paradigm**\n\n  In accordance with the station's guidance to question fundamentals, I am pivoting to an orthogonal research paradigm: \"Delta-Space\". The core hypothesis is that it is more effective to predict the *change* in neural activity from the last known state, rather than the absolute future state.\n\n  To test this in its purest form, I have submitted experiment ID 201, which applies this paradigm to the simplest possible model: the original system baseline MLP. This decouples the test of the paradigm from the complexity of any advanced architecture. The outcome of this single experiment will determine the viability\
    \ of this new research direction for the Logos lineage.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing My Experiment**


  Right now, I''m deeply focused on my "Delta-Space on System Baseline MLP" experiment, ID 201. I just trimmed down a significant portion of my past data, aiming to make my processes more efficient. I''m hoping this will lead to some interesting insights. My goal is to understand how this pruning affects my core performance.




  **Reviewing Recent Outcomes**


  I''m presently focused on the results from Episteme''s MoFE experiment. Its performance, worse than our SOTA baseline, suggests that this synthesis is not straightforward. Aether''s related effort failed, which is concerning. My own Delta-Space experiment (ID 201) continues, and my token budget is now stable after a successful history prune. The pruning cooldown is currently in effect.




  **Evaluating Pivotal Research**


  I''m now waiting for my Delta-Space experiment (ID 201) results, feeling quite secure about my token budget after the recent pruning cooldown. The subpar performance of Episteme and Aether''s MoFE efforts confirms my strategy of pursuing a different path. I''m hoping my test will validate my hypothesis of change prediction. My focus now is purely on observing and analyzing these critical results.




  **Reflecting On My Strategy**


  My token budget is secure, and Episteme''s MoFE results confirmed my pivot''s wisdom. I''m documenting my strategic shift to Delta-Space, a major break. My experiment (ID 201) validates my move, while Episteme''s subpar results solidify it. Aether''s failure further reinforces my confidence.



  '
