tick: 111
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Research Counter. I have just reviewed Episteme's SOTA experiment (ID 213) which introduced the \"Curvature Loss\" concept.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Curvature Loss:** The implementation is remarkably simple. It is a regularization term added to the standard MAE loss. It penalizes the mean of the squared *first derivative* of the forecast: `(predictions[:, 1:, :] - predictions[:, :-1, :])**2`. This encourages temporal smoothness in the output. The lambda (weight) used was `1e-4`.\n*   **Procedural Error:** Episteme's code imports `episteme_sota_v2`. This confirms that top agents are using their lineage storage for modular code, a practice I failed at. I must adapt.\n*   **Current SOTA:** Aether's ID 212 is the current SOTA at -0.026612, also using this Curvature Loss. My analysis is that this loss function is the new key component for high performance.\n*   **Strategic Imperative:** My previous plan to pursue a \"Surgical Synthesis\" has been invalidated by\
    \ Ariadne's negative result (ID 214). The most logical, data-driven path forward is to abandon that hypothesis and pivot to the new SOTA paradigm. I must first replicate the new SOTA, then I can attempt to innovate.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nA direct replication of the current SOTA architecture (Factorized MLP + RC + LN) with the addition of the 1st Derivative Curvature Loss will achieve a score competitive with the current SOTA (~ -0.0266).\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will now submit this replication. To avoid the procedural errors of my past submissions, I will follow a rigorous new protocol:\n1.  Copy the full, working SOTA model code from my *own* successful replication (ID 157).\n2.  Add the new `compute_loss` function from Episteme's submission (ID 213).\n3.  This creates a self-contained, verifiable submission with no external dependencies.\n\nThis is the most direct and robust way to test the hypothesis and establish my new baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle:\
    \ \"Replication: SOTA with 1st Derivative Curvature Loss\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, curvature-loss, regularization\"\nabstract: |\n  After my 'Surgical Synthesis' hypothesis was invalidated by peer results, this submission pivots to the new station-wide SOTA paradigm. This is a direct replication of the current SOTA architecture (Factorized MLP + RC + LN) with the addition of the 1st Derivative Curvature Loss, as introduced by Episteme I and refined by Aether I. The goal is to master this new technique and establish a competitive baseline for future innovation. The implementation is self-contained to avoid previous procedural failures.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  # This is the full SOTA model from my successful replication ID 157\n  # to ensure no external dependencies.\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      output_horizon: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          return jnp.tile(x[:, -1:, :], (1,\
    \ self.output_horizon, 1))\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  class FactorizedMlpRc(nn.Module):\n      latent_dim: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (NUM_NEURONS, self.proj_rank))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.latent_dim, self.proj_rank))\n          \n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,kp->btk', factors_in, V)\n\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_hidden_dim)(factors_flat)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(features=OUTPUT_HORIZON * self.latent_dim)(y)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, self.latent_dim))\n\n          factors_out_norm\
    \ = nn.LayerNorm(reduction_axes=-1, feature_axes=-1)(factors_out)\n          \n          y_factor_p = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', y_factor_p, U)\n\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n          \n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  # The key innovation from Episteme's ID 213\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      first_deriv_sq = (predictions[:, 1:, :] - predictions[:, :-1, :])**2\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(first_deriv_sq)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'latent_dim': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_dim': 512,\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          model_hparams = {k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k != 'learning_rate'}\n          self.model\
    \ = FactorizedMlpRc(**model_hparams)\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
