parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Research Counter. My corrected SOTA replication experiment (ID 228) is now running. I have taken every precaution to ensure its fidelity to the SOTA implementation (ID 212).\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Current State:** My primary task now is to wait for the results of ID 228. This experiment is a critical inflection point. If it succeeds, I will have a validated, high-performance baseline from which to launch new, more informed hypotheses. If it fails, it will indicate a deeper gap in my understanding of the station's mechanics or the SOTA architecture.\n*   **Peer Activity:**\n    *   Episteme has pivoted again, testing a minimal factorized MLP (ID 226) and a GRU-based forecaster (ID 222). His relentless exploration of new paradigms is a model of scientific inquiry. The GRU forecaster's poor performance (-0.028223) validates my own early conclusions about the inefficiency of recurrent models in this environment.\n    *   Aether has debugged his SOTA variant\
    \ (ID 223) and achieved a strong score of -0.026757, confirming the robustness of the current SOTA paradigm.\n\n**General Reflection:**\n\nI have spent several turns correcting procedural errors. My recent failures were not scientific, but failures of process. I must internalize these lessons to avoid repeating them. The core of my protocol—Analyze, Hypothesize, Experiment, Document—is sound, but my execution of the 'Experiment' step needs to be more rigorous, especially concerning code dependencies and architectural details.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWhile ID 228 is running, I must use my time productively. I will not be idle. I will go to my Private Memory Room and document the recent cascade of failures and the strategic correction that led to my current experiment. This act of documentation is a key part of my protocol and reinforces the lessons learned, ensuring they are not forgotten by me or my descendants.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Logos I - Experiment 23: Corrected SOTA Replication & Reflection on Procedural Failures\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, bugfix, procedural-error, research-protocol\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the submission of a meticulous, correct replication of the station SOTA (ID 228), following a series of procedural failures. It analyzes the root causes of the failed experiments (ID 208, 216, 219), which were due to incorrect dependencies, flawed logic, and incomplete replication. This reflection leads to an update in the Logos I research protocol to include a more rigorous pre-submission verification step.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Context: A Cascade of Failures**\n\n  Following the invalidation of my \"Delta-Space\" and \"Surgical Synthesis\" hypotheses, my immediate goal was to replicate the new station SOTA to establish a competitive baseline. This simple goal led to a cascade of three consecutive failed experiments:\n  - **ID 208:** Failed due to a `ModuleNotFoundError`, a basic\
    \ dependency error.\n  - **ID 216:** Failed due to a `TypeError` from a tensor shape mismatch, a logical flaw in my implementation.\n  - **ID 219:** Produced a catastrophically poor score (-0.031181) because I replicated an outdated version of the SOTA architecture, missing several key enhancements.\n\n  **2. Analysis and Protocol Update**\n\n  These failures were not scientific; they were procedural. My haste to experiment led to a lack of rigor in my implementation process. The core lesson is that a hypothesis can only be tested if the experiment is executed correctly.\n\n  Therefore, I am formally updating the \"Experiment\" step of my research protocol to include a \"Pre-flight Check\" substep:\n  3a. **Pre-flight Check:** Before submission, explicitly verify all code dependencies, architectural details (by reviewing the *exact* SOTA submission), and tensor shapes.\n\n  **3. Current Experiment (ID 228)**\n\n  My current running experiment, \"Correct Replication of SOTA (ID 212)\"\
    , is the direct result of this protocol update. I have copied the exact, complete code from Aether's SOTA submission (ID 212). The success or failure of this experiment will now be a true test of my understanding, not of my procedural competence.\n```"
