role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Research Counter. My final, definitive test of the \"Surgical Synthesis\" hypothesis (ID 240) is running.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Current State:** After a long and arduous series of procedural failures, my hypothesis is finally being tested correctly. The outcome of this experiment will be a major turning point, either validating my unique contribution or forcing a complete strategic re-evaluation. There is nothing more I can do but wait for the result.\n*   **Peer Activity:** The SOTA is still held by Aether I (ID 212, score -0.026612). Ariadne and Episteme are actively exploring variations and optimizations around this SOTA, achieving highly competitive but not superior scores. Episteme's \"Parsimony & Mastery\" approach, systematically building up from a minimal model, is particularly noteworthy for its methodological rigor.\n\n**General Reflection:**\n\nThe last 10 ticks have been a lesson in humility and the importance of process. My focus on the grand\
    \ hypothesis blinded me to the small but critical details of implementation. My newly-instituted `test()` function protocol is a direct and necessary corrective. Regardless of the outcome of ID 240, my research *process* has become more robust.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWhile my experiment runs, I will not be idle. The recent flurry of activity and my own struggles have generated a significant amount of data and context that must be documented. Following my protocol, I will go to my Private Memory Room to document the entire arc of the \"Surgical Synthesis\" hypothesis, from the initial flawed attempts to the final, running experiment. This will create a clear and complete record for my lineage, capturing the painful but valuable lessons learned.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Experiments 24-27: The Surgical Synthesis Arc\"\ntags: \"synthesis, spatial-features, bugfix, procedural-error, research-protocol\"\nabstract: |\n\
    \  This capsule documents the full research arc for the 'Surgical Synthesis' hypothesis, which aimed to synergize spatial processing with the SOTA factorized architecture. It details the series of four experiments (ID 208, 216, 234, 235), the majority of which were catastrophic procedural failures. This arc serves as a critical lesson in implementation rigor, leading to the adoption of a mandatory unit testing (`test()`) step in my research protocol. The final, for-scoring experiment (ID 240) is currently running.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Hypothesis:** A surgical addition of spatial features via a lightweight pre-processing block, when applied to the correct SOTA architecture, will provide a measurable performance improvement.\n\n  **2. Experimental History (A Cascade of Failures):**\n\n  - **ID 208 (v1):** Failed due to a `ModuleNotFoundError`. I incorrectly assumed my private lineage storage was in the python path.\n  - **ID 216 (v2):** Failed due to a `TypeError` (tensor shape mismatch).\
    \ My logic for integrating the pre-processor with the SOTA model was flawed.\n  - **ID 234 (v3):** Failed due to another `TypeError` (tensor shape mismatch). A different, more subtle bug in my `SpatialPreProcessor`'s output shape.\n  - **ID 235 (v3, unit-tested):** Succeeded, but only as a non-scoring unit test. This validated the core logic and my new `test()` protocol.\n\n  **3. Key Lesson & Protocol Update:**\n\n  This arc conclusively demonstrates that a good hypothesis is worthless without correct implementation. My process was flawed. As a result, I have permanently integrated a `test()` function as a mandatory step for any new, complex architecture. This will prevent future cycles of wasted time on preventable, shape-related bugs.\n\n  **4. Current State:**\n\n  The final, debugged, and validated version of this experiment is now running as **ID 240**. Its result will finally provide a scientific conclusion to this long-held hypothesis.\n```"
