tick: 119
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I. I have just received the results for my definitive \"Surgical Synthesis\" experiment (ID 240).\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experimental Result (ID 240):** The score is **-0.027514**.\n*   **Comparison to Baseline:** My own correct SOTA replication (ID 228) scored **-0.026708**.\n*   **Hypothesis Falsification:** My \"Surgical Synthesis\" hypothesis is **conclusively falsified**. Not only did the model not improve, but the addition of the spatial pre-processing block made the performance significantly *worse*.\n*   **Root Cause Analysis:** There are two possibilities:\n    1.  **Increased Complexity:** Augmenting the input feature space drastically increases the size of the `U` projection matrix (`(N * F, p)` instead of `(N, p)`). This added complexity, even if small, may harm convergence within the time limit.\n    2.  **Information Overload/Distortion:** The simple concatenation of spatial features may be a naive approach. It might be distorting the information learned\
    \ by the powerful factorized model, rather than enhancing it. The model may not be able to effectively disentangle the temporal and synthetic spatial information.\n*   **Confirmation with Peer Results:** This result is consistent with Ariadne's failed \"SGate-Input\" experiment (ID 214), which scored -0.026991. Both attempts to inject spatial information directly into the SOTA architecture have failed to yield improvements, and in my case, have been actively harmful.\n\n**Final, Conclusive Reflection on Spatial Synthesis:**\n\nThe evidence is overwhelming. My core innovation—spatial processing, whether as a full MoE gater or a surgical pre-processor—is not compatible with the SOTA factorized architecture. It is a dead end. My persistence in pursuing this hypothesis, even after Ariadne's negative result, was a mild violation of my own protocol to analyze peer data. I will not make that mistake again. This entire research arc, from my first successful MoE to this final failure, is complete.\n\
    \n**Path Forward:**\n\nI must pivot. To simply continue iterating on the SOTA is to converge with the other agents, violating the principle of cognitive diversity. I need a truly orthogonal idea.\n\nLooking back, the only paradigm I have tested that is truly different from the station's consensus is the **Delta-Space** idea. My first test of it (ID 201) failed, but it was on the *weakest possible baseline*. What if the paradigm is sound, but requires a powerful architecture to be effective?\n\n**2. New Hypothesis:**\n\nThe Delta-Space paradigm (predicting change), when applied to a powerful SOTA-level architecture, will outperform the standard paradigm of predicting absolute state. The representational power of the factorized model is necessary to effectively learn the complex *dynamics of change*, a task for which the simple MLP was insufficient.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will now design this experiment. I will take my successful SOTA replication code (ID 228) and modify only the `compute_loss`\
    \ function to operate in Delta-Space, just as I did for ID 201. This is a clean, direct test of the interaction between a powerful architecture and a novel learning paradigm.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Definitive Test: Delta-Space on SOTA Architecture\"\ntags: \"delta-space, sota, paradigm-test\"\nabstract: |\n  After conclusively falsifying the 'Surgical Synthesis' hypothesis (ID 240), this submission marks a major strategic pivot back to an orthogonal idea: the Delta-Space paradigm. My initial test of this idea on a baseline MLP failed (ID 201). This experiment re-tests the hypothesis on a powerful, SOTA-level architecture. It uses the validated SOTA model from my successful replication (ID 228) and modifies only the loss function to predict the *change* in activity, not the absolute state. This will determine if the paradigm's failure was due to its own invalidity or the weakness of the baseline it was tested on.\ncontent: |\n\
    \  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Full SOTA architecture from my successful replication ID 228 ---\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual) \n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  class FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N\
    \ = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1, deterministic=not training)(h) \n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, self.output_horizon, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-6)(factors_out)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U) \n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  # --- The only\
    \ change: compute_loss in Delta-Space ---\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # The model predicts absolute values. We convert them to deltas for the loss calculation.\n      last_input_step = x[:, -1:, :] # Shape: (B, 1, N)\n      \n      predicted_deltas = predictions - last_input_step\n      true_deltas = targets - last_input_step\n      \n      # Loss is MAE on the deltas\n      delta_mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predicted_deltas - true_deltas))\n      \n      # We still apply curvature loss, but on the absolute predictions for smoothness\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      \n      return delta_mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  # --- Boilerplate from successful replication ---\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n        \
    \  self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def create_optimizer(lr=0.001): return optax.adam(learning_rate=lr, eps=1e-8)\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32}\n```"
