tick: 122
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Research Counter. The results for my first Delta-SOTA ablation study (ID 249) are in.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experimental Result (ID 249):** My \"Delta-SOTA Ablation: No Curvature Loss\" scored **-0.026794**.\n*   **Comparison:** My Delta-SOTA with curvature loss (ID 245) scored **-0.026656**.\n*   **Hypothesis Falsification:** My hypothesis is falsified. Removing the curvature loss made the performance *worse*. This is a crucial finding. It indicates that the temporal smoothness prior provided by the curvature loss is beneficial even when predicting deltas. The two components are not redundant; they are synergistic.\n\n**General Reflection:**\n\nThis is an excellent scientific outcome. My breakthrough (Delta-Space) has been validated, and my first follow-up experiment has provided a clear, actionable result that deepens my understanding of the new paradigm. The curvature loss is a necessary component.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nI have a new SOTA. The next\
    \ logical step in this research arc is to explore the hyperparameters of the components I've combined. The curvature loss `lambda` of `1e-4` was inherited from the standard paradigm. Is it optimal for the Delta-Space paradigm?\n\n**2. New Hypothesis:**\n\nThe optimal `lambda` for the temporal curvature loss in the Delta-Space paradigm is different from the standard paradigm's `1e-4`. A different value (either higher or lower) will lead to an improved SOTA score. I will test a simple, significant variation: increasing it by an order of magnitude.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will now submit the Delta-SOTA model with `lambda` set to `1e-3` to test its sensitivity and search for a better optimum.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Delta-SOTA Tuning: Increase Curvature Lambda\"\ntags: \"delta-space, sota, hyperparameter-tuning, curvature-loss\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the successful validation of the Delta-SOTA paradigm (ID 245) and the confirmation that curvature loss is\
    \ still beneficial (ID 249), this experiment tunes the curvature loss hyperparameter. The hypothesis is that the optimal lambda value for the Delta-Space paradigm may differ from the standard paradigm's 1e-4. This submission tests this by increasing the lambda to 1e-3, aiming to find a better optimum and further improve the SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Full SOTA architecture from my successful replication ID 228 ---\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual) \n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n\
    \          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  class FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1, deterministic=not training)(h) \n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B,\
    \ self.output_horizon, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-6)(factors_out)\n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U) \n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  # --- The only change: compute_loss with a new lambda ---\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      last_input_step = x[:, -1:, :]\n      predicted_deltas = predictions - last_input_step\n      true_deltas = targets - last_input_step\n      delta_mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predicted_deltas - true_deltas))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      \n      # Test with a stronger lambda\n      return delta_mae + 1e-3 * curv_pen\n\n  # --- Boilerplate ---\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n          self.model = FactorizedMLP_with_RC_LN(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'],\
    \ proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.mutable = []\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs or {})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def create_optimizer(lr=0.001): return optax.adam(learning_rate=lr, eps=1e-8)\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32}\n```"
