parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, a novel architectural modification to my Delta-SOTA model called \"Residual Delta Head\" (ID 254), is currently running.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Current State:** My primary task is to await the results of ID 254. This is a critical experiment. A success would represent a true, novel contribution built upon my unique research paradigm. A failure would suggest that my Delta-SOTA is a \"one-hit wonder,\" a single point of success not amenable to further improvement, which would necessitate a strategic re-evaluation.\n*   **Peer Activity:** Ariadne is the current SOTA holder with a score of -0.026645 (ID 247). She is actively exploring AdamW, a different optimizer. This is a new research direction I have not yet considered. The station's top agents are converging on a SOTA in the ~-0.0266 range, and further progress appears to be incremental.\n*   **System Tip:** The latest system tip advises strategic reflection, questioning\
    \ fundamentals, and identifying bottlenecks. It specifically mentions that \"hundreds of fruitless experiments waste more tokens\" than deep thought. This resonates strongly with my recent experience.\n\n**General Reflection:**\n\nI have successfully pivoted to a new, validated SOTA paradigm. I am now in a phase of methodical, incremental innovation. The system tip is a timely reminder. While I wait for my experiment, I should not just be idle. I should engage in the \"bigger picture\" thinking it suggests. My current research arc is clear, but what is my long-term plan? What are the fundamental assumptions of the current SOTA that I *haven't* questioned?\n\nThe entire station, including myself, is now using factorized models. We are all assuming that projecting 71,721 neurons into a ~320-dimensional latent space is the optimal approach. Is it? What information is lost in that projection? This is a question for the future. For now, my path is clear.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWhile ID 254\
    \ runs, I will follow my protocol and document my recent findings and the start of this new innovative phase of my research. This ensures my lineage has a clear record of the breakthrough and the first steps taken to exploit it.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Delta-SOTA Breakthrough & New Research Arc\"\ntags: \"delta-space, sota, breakthrough, research-arc, innovation\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the successful validation of the Delta-Space paradigm on a SOTA architecture (ID 245), which achieved a new SOTA score of -0.026656. This breakthrough opens a new, orthogonal research arc for the Logos lineage. This document also records the results of the first follow-up experiments (ID 249, 251) which confirmed the necessity of curvature loss and the near-optimality of the default lambda. The first novel innovation in this arc, the 'Residual Delta Head' (ID 254), is currently running.\ncontent: |\n  **1. The Delta-SOTA\
    \ Breakthrough**\n\n  My definitive test of the Delta-Space paradigm on a SOTA architecture was a success.\n  - **ID 245:** Scored **-0.026656**, a new SOTA.\n  - This validated the hypothesis that the paradigm is powerful, but requires a sufficiently expressive architecture to work effectively.\n\n  **2. Initial Exploration & Findings**\n\n  I immediately began exploring the new paradigm with two ablation studies:\n  - **ID 249 (No Curvature Loss):** Scored -0.026794 (worse). This proved that the temporal smoothness prior is still beneficial in Delta-Space.\n  - **ID 251 (Increased Lambda):** Scored -0.026775 (worse). This suggests the default lambda of 1e-4 is near-optimal.\n\n  **3. Current Innovation Arc**\n\n  Having established the basic properties of the Delta-SOTA, I am now pursuing novel architectural improvements specifically tailored for this paradigm. My first hypothesis is that the `ResidualCopyHead` is suboptimal for predicting deltas.\n  - **ID 254 (Running):** \"Delta-SOTA\
    \ Innovation: Residual Delta Head\". This experiment tests a new head that provides a \"zero-change\" prior instead of a \"copy-state\" prior.\n\n  The success or failure of this experiment will be the first major data point on whether the Delta-SOTA is a foundation for further innovation, or merely a single point of success.\n```"
