tick: 137
role: model
parts:
- text: "**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **My Experiment Result (ID 278):** My Synthesis SOTA model has returned a score of **-0.026781**.\n    *   **Comparison to SOTA:** The current global SOTA is -0.026573 (Aether I, ID 259). My score is very close, but not superior.\n    *   **Comparison to my previous best:** My previous best *robust* score was -0.027756 (ID 65). This is a massive improvement of nearly 0.001, a huge leap in performance.\n    *   **Hypothesis Validation:** My hypothesis is **strongly validated**. Synthesizing the best practices from my peers has created an extremely powerful and competitive model. While not a new global SOTA, it has successfully brought my own research lineage to the absolute cutting edge. My new baseline is now formidable. The debugger had to fix another RNG handling bug, which reinforces the value of robust code design.\n\n*   **MoE Literature Review:** My `filter` command has revealed a rich history of MoE experiments from Aether I and Episteme I.\n    * \
    \  **Community Convergence:** Aether and Episteme collaborated on a \"Mixture of Factorized Experts\" (MoFE) (ID 200, 206), achieving scores around -0.0274. This is a powerful idea but did not beat the simpler, single-expert models.\n    *   **My Previous Attempts:** My own attempts to create MoE versions of the SOTA (ID 134, 144) also fell into this -0.0271 to -0.0273 range.\n    *   **The MoE Bottleneck:** There is a clear pattern: applying MoE to the full, complex expert models is too computationally expensive. The models don't have enough time to train, and their performance is capped in the low -0.027s, which is now significantly behind the SOTA. My \"Grand Synthesis\" hypothesis from tick 71 was likely flawed for this reason.\n\n**Synthesis and New Path Forward:**\n\nI have successfully reached the SOTA performance tier. My goal is now to surpass it. The literature review on MoE has been illuminating: simply wrapping the SOTA model in an MoE framework is a dead end due to computational\
    \ cost.\n\nA new, more surgical approach is needed. What if, instead of having multiple full-sized expert models, we have multiple *components* inside a single model, selected by a gating network?\n\nThe most parameter-heavy and influential components of the SOTA model are the factor loading matrices, `U` and `V`. All information about neural identity and latent space structure is contained within them.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\n**Hypothesis:** A Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) model, where a gating network selects between multiple pairs of `U` and `V` matrices within a single factorized architecture, will be more computationally efficient than a full Mixture of Experts. This efficiency will allow for sufficient training, and the specialized factor loadings will capture the different experimental conditions more effectively than a single set of loadings, leading to a new SOTA.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will design a \"MoFL\" model.\n1.  **Architecture:** It will be based on my new\
    \ Synthesis SOTA model (ID 278).\n2.  **Gating:** It will use my proven spatial CNN gater.\n3.  **Mixture Mechanism:** Instead of multiple complete `SynthesizedSotaModel` experts, I will have one model, but the `U` and `V` parameters will be defined as a stack of `num_experts` matrices. The gating network will compute weights, and the final `U` and `V` used for projection will be a weighted average of the expert `U`'s and `V`'s. This moves the \"mixture\" from the output level to the parameter level.\n\nThis is a novel, parsimonious, and direct test of a new idea. I will document my successful synthesis and this new plan.\n\n`/execute_action{unfilter}`\n`/execute_action{page_size 10}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Synthesis Arc (Part 2): Success & New Hypothesis\"\ntags: \"synthesis, sota-replication, hypothesis-validated, mixture-of-factor-loadings\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the successful conclusion of\
    \ the Synthesis Arc. Experiment ID 278 achieved a score of -0.026781, bringing my research to the SOTA performance tier. A review of MoE history revealed computational bottlenecks, leading to a new, more parsimonious hypothesis.\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc Conclusion: Synthesis of Best Practices**\n\n  **Experiment 2: Synthesis SOTA v2 (ID 278)**\n  - **Score:** -0.026781\n  - **Result:** A massive improvement over my previous best, and competitive with the global SOTA (-0.026573).\n  - **Conclusion:** The hypothesis is **validated**. Synthesizing the SOTA components (deeper MLP, AdamW, clipping) is the correct approach and has established a new, powerful baseline for my lineage.\n\n  **Analysis of MoE History:**\n  A review of all \"mixture-of-experts\" submissions shows a performance ceiling around -0.0272 to -0.0274. The computational cost of running multiple full SOTA experts prevents them from training effectively.\n\n  **New Hypothesis: Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL)**\n \
    \ To overcome the MoE bottleneck, I hypothesize that applying the mixture mechanism to the parameter level, specifically the `U` and `V` factor loading matrices, will be more efficient. A gating network will select a weighted average of several `(U, V)` pairs within a single model. This targeted specialization should capture different data conditions without the prohibitive cost of full expert duplication. This is my next major research arc.\n```"
