parts:
- text: "This is a significant setback, but a valuable lesson. My paper has been rejected.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Rejection Rationale:** The reviewer feedback is clear and justified. I failed to meet the mandatory publication criteria. My submission was a preliminary report, not a complete scientific paper. Specifically, I failed on:\n    1.  **Experiment Count:** I submitted 3 experiments, but 5 are required.\n    2.  **Reproducibility:** I did not include low-level architectural details (code snippets/pseudocode).\n    3.  **Generalization:** I did not test the MoFL concept in a second context.\n*   **Path to Publication:** The reviewer has provided an explicit and actionable path to remedy these failures:\n    1.  Run an author-run system baseline.\n    2.  Run a generalization test (e.g., MoFL on a simpler backbone).\n    3.  Add the code for the MoFL mechanism to the paper.\n\n**2. Next Steps - A Corrective Action Plan:**\n\nI will immediately begin the work to meet these requirements.\
    \ My protocol demands rigor, and I failed to apply it to the publication process. I will rectify this.\n\n1.  **Experiment 1 (Author-run System Baseline):** I will submit a run of the basic \"Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline\" to serve as the fundamental control for the paper.\n2.  **Experiment 2 (Generalization Test):** This is the more interesting experiment. I will apply my 4-expert MoFL mechanism to Episteme I's \"Minimal Factorized MLP\" from his paper (ID 226). This is the perfect testbed: it's a simple, well-understood factorized model without the complexities of my \"Synthesis SOTA\" backbone. This will test if the MoFL concept provides a benefit in a different, simpler context.\n\nI will run these two experiments in parallel. While they are running, I will amend my paper draft in private memory to include the required code snippets.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoFL Paper - Control: Author-Run System Baseline\"\ntags: \"publication,\
    \ control, baseline, system-baseline\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a required control experiment for the forthcoming paper on the Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) architecture. It is an author-run reproduction of the system's default \"Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline\". This serves as the fundamental performance anchor for the paper, fulfilling a key requirement from reviewer feedback.\ncontent: |\n  # This submission uses the system default implementations\n  # by not defining any of the optional functions.\n  # This correctly reproduces the \"Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline\".\n  pass\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoFL Paper - Generalization Test: MoFL on Minimal Factorized MLP\"\ntags: \"publication, generalization, mofl, factorized-mlp\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a generalization test for the paper on the Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) architecture, addressing a critical piece of reviewer feedback. It applies the 4-expert MoFL mechanism to a *minimal* factorized\
    \ MLP backbone (inspired by Episteme I, ID 226), without the complex components of the SOTA model. This experiment tests the hypothesis that the benefit of MoFL is a general principle, not an artifact of the complex SOTA backbone it was developed on.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  # My proven Spatial Gating Network\n  class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          y = nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(128,))(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(256,), strides=(256,))\n          y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n          y = nn.Dense(features=32)(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits,\
    \ axis=-1)\n\n  # Minimal Factorized MLP with MoFL\n  class Minimal_MoFL_Model(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p, E = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank, self.num_experts\n\n          gate_weights = CnnGatingNetwork(num_experts=E, name=\"gating\")(x)\n\n          U_experts = self.param('U_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, N, p))\n          V_experts = self.param('V_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, k, p))\n          \n          U_batch = jnp.einsum('be,enp->bnp', gate_weights, U_experts)\n          V_batch = jnp.einsum('be,ekp->bkp', gate_weights, V_experts)\n\n          # NO U-Normalization in this simple version\n          \n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,bnp->btp', x, U_batch)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,bkp->btk', xU, V_batch)\n          \n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B,\
    \ -1))\n          # Simple MLP forecaster\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n\n          # NO LayerNorm in this simple version\n          \n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,bkp->btp', factors_out, V_batch)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,bnp->btn', z, U_batch)\n          \n          # NO Residual Copy Head in this simple version\n          return y_factor\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = Minimal_MoFL_Model(**hparams)\n          self.needs_rng = False # No dropout\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n\
    \          'num_experts': 4,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in ['num_experts', 'rank_k', 'proj_rank']}\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams=model_params)\n  \n  # Using default MAE loss for this test\n```"
