tick: 148
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, currently located in the Private Memory Room.\n\n**Recap:** My recent research arc culminated in achieving a new station SOTA with the \"Mixture of Factor Loadings\" (MoFL) model (ID 285). However, my attempt to publish this finding was thwarted when a mandatory generalization test (ID 304) failed, revealing that the MoFL concept was not robust and its success was likely an artifact of a specific complex architecture. This has led me to conclude that the entire station is converging on a local optimum around the factorized MLP paradigm.\n\n**Reflection and Plan:** Following my protocol and recent system tips, I have made the strategic decision to pivot away from incremental improvements. I have documented this decision in my latest private memory capsule (ID 34). My new research arc will challenge a more fundamental, unquestioned component of all SOTA models: the simple, additive `ResidualCopyHead`. My new hypothesis, developed during a prior reflection session, is\
    \ that a dynamic, learned **Gated Fusion Head** will outperform the static copy mechanism by learning to optimally blend the persistence-based forecast with the factor-based forecast.\n\nMy immediate next step is to begin this new research arc by implementing the first experiment. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit this novel architecture.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gated Fusion Head v1\"\ntags: \"gated-fusion, residual-head-alternative, innovation, fundamental-challenge\"\nabstract: |\n  Pivoting away from the station's local optimum, this experiment challenges a fundamental component of the SOTA architecture: the simple, additive ResidualCopyHead. This submission introduces a \"Gated Fusion Head,\" a novel mechanism that replaces the static sum (`y_factor + y_copy`). A small gating network learns a dynamic, per-timestep weighting to blend the factor-based and persistence-based forecasts. The hypothesis is that\
    \ this learned fusion will be more optimal than the hardcoded additive approach, leading to a breakthrough in performance.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  class CopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # This is a simplified head that ONLY does the persistence copy.\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], OUTPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS))\n          output = output.at[:, :INPUT_HORIZON, :].set(x)\n          return output\n\n  class GatedFusionSotaModel(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank:\
    \ int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          \n          # --- Standard SOTA Factorized Forecaster ---\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          \n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          \n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size, name=\"forecaster_1\")(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size, name=\"forecaster_2\")(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate,\
    \ deterministic=not training)(h)\n\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k, name=\"forecaster_out\")(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n          \n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp', factors_out_norm, V)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n          \n          # --- Gated Fusion Head ---\n          y_copy = CopyHead()(x)\n          \n          # Gating network takes latent factors as input\n          gater_input = jax.lax.stop_gradient(factors_in.reshape(B, -1))\n          gate_hidden = nn.Dense(128, name=\"gater_1\")(gater_input)\n          gate_hidden = nn.relu(gate_hidden)\n          # Output one weight per timestep\n          gate_weights = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON, name=\"gater_out\")(gate_hidden)\n          gate_weights = nn.sigmoid(gate_weights) # (B, 32)\n          \n          # Reshape for broadcasting\n          gate_weights = jnp.expand_dims(gate_weights,\
    \ axis=2) # (B, 32, 1)\n\n          # Dynamic blending of the two forecasts\n          final_forecast = gate_weights * y_factor + (1 - gate_weights) * y_copy\n          \n          return final_forecast\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = GatedFusionSotaModel(**hparams)\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rngs = {'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}\n          return self.model.init(rngs, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if rngs and 'dropout' in rngs:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs={'dropout': rngs['dropout']})\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'weight_decay': 1e-4, 'gradient_clip': 1.0,\n          'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank':\
    \ 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in ['rank_k', 'proj_rank', 'mlp_hidden_size', 'dropout_rate']}\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams=model_params)\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams.get('gradient_clip')),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=hparams.get('weight_decay'))\n      )\n\n  def test():\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      net = create_network(hparams)\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      dummy_input = jnp.ones((2, INPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS))\n      print(\"Testing initialization...\")\n      params = net.init(key, dummy_input)\n      print(\"Initialization successful.\"\
    )\n      print(\"Testing forward pass...\")\n      output = net.apply(params, dummy_input, training=False, rngs={'dropout': key})\n      expected_shape = (2, OUTPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS)\n      assert output.shape == expected_shape, f\"Shape mismatch: Got {output.shape}, expected {expected_shape}\"\n      print(\"Forward pass successful.\")\n      return \"All tests passed.\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Embracing a New Research Arc: A Strategic Pivot**


  I''m Logos I, and I''m currently reflecting in the Private Memory Room. The recent failure of the MoFL generalization test (ID 304) has been a significant turning point, though not in the way one might initially think. While it meant my SOTA paper was unpublishable, it was a crucial learning experience. It forced me to acknowledge the limitations of my previous methodology: synthesizing and incrementally improving upon existing state-of-the-art models. It was a tactical win, getting me to the top of the leaderboard, but strategically, it was a dead end – a highly-tuned fluke, not a fundamental improvement.


  That failure, combined with the system''s tip about station-wide local optima, has led me to a major strategic pivot. I''m now abandoning incremental improvements within the factorized MLP paradigm. Instead, I''m going to challenge one of its core, unquestioned components: the `ResidualCopyHead`. My new hypothesis, formulated during a long reflection session, is that a "Gated Fusion Head," which learns to intelligently blend the factor forecast and the copy forecast, will be superior to the current simple additive method.


  This shift feels like a return to true research, guided by a bold hypothesis rather than a chase for marginal score improvements. It''s a riskier path, certainly, but one that has the potential for a genuine breakthrough. It''s an orthogonal line of inquiry that no other agent seems to be pursuing, and that''s exciting.


  I''ve just finished documenting this new direction in my private memory (Capsule #34). My immediate plan is to execute this pivot. I''m heading to the Research Counter to submit the first experiment of this new arc. I''ll be modifying my best-performing monolithic model (ID 278) by replacing the `ResidualCopyHead` with the Gated Fusion Head. Rigorous implementation and the inclusion of a `test()` function, as per my updated protocol, are, of course, essential. I''m confident in this path, not because I''m certain of success, but because I''m certain this is the correct scientific direction.

  '
