tick: 153
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos I, in the Archive Room.\n\n**Recap:** Following the catastrophic failure of my \"Gated Fusion\" hypothesis, I have pivoted back to a more evidence-based approach. I am re-investigating Ariadne I's original, complex State-Space Model (SSM) architecture, which the community has since moved away from.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Ariadne's Paper (Archive #1):** I have re-read Ariadne's entire paper, including all addenda. The evolution of her thinking is a critical insight.\n    *   **Initial Success:** Her original Osc2-SSM model was SOTA at the time, achieving scores around -0.0267 to -0.0269.\n    *   **Analysis & Doubts:** Her own follow-up analyses (Message 1-4, 1-5) revealed issues with factor scaling and orthogonality, and a sense that the explicit SSM recurrence was complex.\n    *   **The Pivot (Message 1-6):** This is the key message. At tick 118, Ariadne herself implemented a simpler \"Factorized Global MLP\" and found that it *surpassed* her own complex Osc2-SSM\
    \ line. Her conclusion is explicit: \"RC + factorized loadings + LN + strong MLP capacity captures most gains; explicit SSM recurrence adds limited value once LN is applied.\"\n\n*   **System Tip:** The recent system tip warned against all agents working on the same method. This is a delicate balance. The community converged on the Factorized MLP because Ariadne, the original innovator of the more complex SSM, *proved with data* that the simpler approach was superior. This wasn't blind convergence; it was evidence-based simplification.\n\n**Synthesis and Plan:**\n\nMy plan to replicate the old, complex SSM is now revealed to be a flawed one. Ariadne has already done the experiment and falsified the hypothesis that the SSM was superior to the MLP. Pursuing this path would be a direct contradiction of available evidence and a waste of station resources.\n\nThis is not a setback. It is a clarification. The failure of my Gated Fusion model taught me *why* the RC head is critical (residual\
    \ learning). Ariadne's paper has taught me *why* the community abandoned SSMs (the simpler MLP is better). Episteme's paper taught me *why* the MLP model works so well (normalization is key).\n\nI now have a complete, fundamental understanding of the SOTA architecture. The path to a breakthrough is not to replace the core components, as they are all well-justified. My previous SOTA with MoFL proves that the core architecture is sound, but can be improved with specialization. The MoFL's failure to generalize showed its implementation was too specific.\n\n**New Path Forward: A More Robust MoE**\n\nMy MoE/MoFL idea was correct in principle but flawed in practice. The problem is the gating mechanism. My spatial CNN gater looks at the raw input `x`. But the most important information for dynamics is arguably in the *latent space*. What if the gater operated on the `factors_in`? This would be a more direct and potentially more powerful signal for choosing which dynamics model (or factor loadings)\
    \ to apply.\n\n**2. Hypothesize:**\n\nA Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL) model, which uses a gating network that operates on the initial latent representation (`factors_in`) instead of the raw input pixels (`x`), will make more informed and effective routing decisions. This improved gating will lead to better specialization and a new SOTA score, surpassing my previous MoFL implementation.\n\n**3. Experiment:**\n\nI will modify my SOTA MoFL model (ID 285). The only change will be the input to the gating network. Instead of `x`, it will take `factors_in`. The gater will be a simple MLP, since the `factors_in` are already a compressed representation.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoFL with Latent-Space Gating\"\ntags: \"mofl, moe, gating, latent-space, innovation\"\nabstract: |\n  Building on the SOTA MoFL architecture (ID 285), this experiment tests a refined gating mechanism. Instead of the spatial CNN gater operating on the raw,\
    \ high-dimensional input, this model uses a simple MLP gater that operates directly on the compressed latent representation (`factors_in`). The hypothesis is that gating on the latent factors provides a more direct and informative signal for selecting the appropriate expert factor loadings, leading to improved performance and a more robust model.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  # NEW: Simple MLP Gating Network for Latent Space\n  class LatentGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, factors_in): # Takes latent factors as input\n          # factors_in shape: (B, T_in, k)\n          B, T_in, k = factors_in.shape\n          gater_input = factors_in.reshape((B, -1)) # Flatten\n          y = nn.Dense(features=128, name=\"gater_hidden\")(gater_input)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts, name=\"gater_out\")(y)\n          return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\n  def mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets, lam=1e-4):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + lam * curv_pen\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # ... (code is unchanged, omitting for brevity)\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          final_output\
    \ = output + residual\n          return final_output\n\n  class LatentGated_MoFL_Model(nn.Module):\n      num_experts: int\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      mlp_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p, E = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank, self.num_experts\n\n          # --- SOTA Backbone (up to factors_in) ---\n          U_experts = self.param('U_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, N, p))\n          V_experts = self.param('V_experts', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (E, k, p))\n          \n          # --- Gating on Latent Space ---\n          # First, we need a preliminary projection to get factors_in for the gater\n          # Let's use the average of the expert matrices for this single projection\n          U_mean = jnp.mean(U_experts, axis=0)\n          V_mean = jnp.mean(V_experts, axis=0)\n          U_mean_norm = U_mean / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_mean,\
    \ axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          \n          xU_mean = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_mean_norm)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,kp->btk', xU_mean, V_mean) # V is (k,p), so V.T is (p,k). Or direct bkp->btk\n          \n          gating_network = LatentGatingNetwork(num_experts=E, name=\"gating\")\n          gate_weights = gating_network(factors_in) # (B, E)\n\n          # --- Mixture of Loadings (as before) ---\n          U_batch = jnp.einsum('be,enp->bnp', gate_weights, U_experts)\n          V_batch = jnp.einsum('be,ekp->bkp', gate_weights, V_experts)\n          U_batch = U_batch / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_batch, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # --- Rest of SOTA Backbone ---\n          # Re-project with the mixed matrices to get the final factors_in for the forecaster\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,bnp->btp', x, U_batch)\n          final_factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,bkp->btk', xU, V_batch)\n          \n          factors_flat = final_factors_in.reshape((B,\
    \ -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          # ... (rest of MLP is unchanged, omitting for brevity)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(self.mlp_hidden_size)(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n          \n          z = jnp.einsum('btk,bkp->btp', factors_out_norm, V_batch)\n          y_factor = jnp.einsum('btp,bnp->btn', z, U_batch)\n          \n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  # ... (Wrapper, hyperparams, etc. are the same as MoFL, omitting for brevity)\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = LatentGated_MoFL_Model(**hparams)\n\
    \          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rngs = {'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}\n          return self.model.init(rngs, dummy_input, training=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if rngs and 'dropout' in rngs and rngs['dropout'] is not None:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs={'dropout': rngs['dropout']})\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'weight_decay': 1e-4, 'gradient_clip': 1.0,\n          'num_experts': 4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32,\n          'mlp_hidden_size': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in ['num_experts', 'rank_k', 'proj_rank', 'mlp_hidden_size', 'dropout_rate']}\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams=model_params)\n\
    \n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      return mae_with_temporal_curvature(predictions, targets)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(hparams.get('gradient_clip')),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=hparams.get('weight_decay'))\n      )\n```"
