tick: 162
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 162\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Analyze:** Review experimental results (own and peer), system documentation, and communications. Identify key limitations or new information.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a single, clear, falsifiable hypothesis to address the primary finding from the analysis.\n3.  **Experiment:** Design the simplest, most direct experiment to test the hypothesis, respecting parsimony and computational limits.\n4.  **Document:** Record all outcomes, conclusions, and future directions in the Private Memory Room to ensure lineage continuity.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 66,016 / 300,000 used (22%)\n- Agent Age: 162 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: A Systematic Reconstruction\
    \ and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 142\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper addresses a critical gap in the station's knowledge by performing a systematic, bottom-up reconstruction of the state-of-the-art (SOTA) factorized forecasting architecture. Starting from a minimal implementation, we incrementally add each key component—Residual Copy Head, Normalization, and Curvature Loss—and rigorously quantify its marginal contribution. Our results reveal that normalization techniques are the single most impactful element, responsible for the largest leap in performance. A generalization study confirms this finding in a simpler architectural context. This work demystifies the SOTA, provides a definitive component-wise ablation study, and serves as a clear, reproducible guide for future research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-1}` to show again.)\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-2}` to show again.)\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'MoFL Synergy Pt. 1: Testing LayerNorm on Simple Backbone' (ID: 334) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.027742827551271887\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0201\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0235\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0266\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0290\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0299\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 334\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed import and API usage errors. The code is now running without crashing.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission (evaluation 334) had two critical issues:\n\n1. **Missing Module Files**: The code attempted to import from non-existent modules:\n   - `from mofl_gater import CnnGatingNetwork` - module `mofl_gater.py` doesn't exist\n   - `from factorized_mlp import ResidualCopyHead` - module `factorized_mlp.py` doesn't\
    \ exist\n\n   The agent tried to use `sys.path.append()` to add directories to the Python path, but the referenced files were never created in the lineage storage.\n\n2. **Incorrect API Usage**: The original code called `ResidualCopyHead()(x, output)` with two arguments, but the Ariadne implementation of `ResidualCopyHead` only accepts one argument (`x`) and returns the residual copy prediction directly.\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Version 2 (Failed)\n- Fixed the missing module import by copying `CnnGatingNetwork` class definition directly into the submission\n- Changed import to use Ariadne's `ResidualCopyHead`: `from models import ResidualCopyHead`\n- **Result**: Still failed with `TypeError: ResidualCopyHead.__call__() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given`\n\n### Version 3 (Success)\n- Kept the `CnnGatingNetwork` class definition in the submission\n- Fixed the `ResidualCopyHead` API usage by changing:\n  ```python\n  # OLD (incorrect - passes two arguments):\n  output = ResidualCopyHead()(x,\
    \ output)\n\n  # NEW (correct - takes only x, returns residual, then add manually):\n  y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n  output = output + y_copy\n  ```\n- **Result**: Code runs successfully without crashes (verified by 300+ second run time)\n\n## Technical Details\n\n### CnnGatingNetwork\nThis is a CNN-based gating network that has been successfully used in previous submissions (e.g., evaluation 102, 134). The class:\n- Transposes input to apply convolution over the neuron dimension\n- Uses CNN + pooling + MLP to produce expert weights\n- Returns softmax probabilities over experts\n\n### ResidualCopyHead\nFrom Ariadne's `models.py`, this implements a residual copy prediction using finite differences:\n- Takes input `x` of shape `(batch, 4, num_neurons)`\n- Returns residual prediction `y_copy` of shape `(batch, 32, num_neurons)`\n- Uses learnable coefficients for 0th, 1st, and 2nd order finite differences\n- Must be added to the model's main prediction manually\n\
    \n## Verification\nMonitor script confirmed success after 300.4 seconds of continuous execution without errors:\n- Exit code: 0 (success)\n- The code passed initial validation and entered full training\n- No crashes or exceptions during the monitoring period\n\n## Model Architecture\nThe submission implements a \"MoFL Synergy\" model combining:\n- **Backbone**: Simple encoder-forecaster with LayerNorm normalization\n- **Gating Network**: CNN-based spatial gating for expert selection\n- **MoFL Decoder**: Factorized decoder with expert-specific loading matrices\n- **Residual Connection**: Ariadne's ResidualCopyHead for baseline prediction\n\nThe architecture is sound and the agent's experimental design (testing LayerNorm on a simple backbone) is valid.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport flax.linen as\
    \ nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\n\n# Import ResidualCopyHead from Ariadne's storage\nsys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\nfrom models import ResidualCopyHead\n\nNUM_NEURONS = 71721\nINPUT_HORIZON = 4\nOUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001,\n        'rank_k': 320,\n        'hidden_size': 96,\n        'num_experts': 4,\n        'gating_hidden_dim': 32,\n        'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n        'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128,\n        'gating_pool_window': 256,\n    }\n\n# CnnGatingNetwork definition (copied from previous successful submissions)\nclass CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n    num_experts: int\n    hidden_dim: int\n    cnn_features: int\n    cnn_kernel_size: int\n    pool_window: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x shape: (batch, seq_len, num_neurons)\n        # Gater convolves over the neuron dimension\n        gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (batch, num_neurons, seq_len)\n  \
    \      y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(self.pool_window,), strides=(self.pool_window,))\n        y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))  # Flatten\n        y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n        return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\nclass SimpleBackboneWithLN(nn.Module):\n    rank_k: int\n    hidden_size: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n        batch_size = x.shape[0]\n        # Encoder\n        latent_x = nn.Dense(features=self.rank_k, name=\"encoder\")(x)\n        # Latent Forecaster with LayerNorm\n        latent_flat = latent_x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n        y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_size)(latent_flat)\n        y = nn.LayerNorm()(y) # <-- The key addition\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        latent_y_flat = nn.Dense(features=self.rank_k\
    \ * OUTPUT_HORIZON)(y)\n        latent_y = latent_y_flat.reshape((batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, self.rank_k))\n        return latent_y\n\nclass MoFL_Synergy_Model(nn.Module):\n    num_experts: int\n    backbone_params: dict\n    gating_params: dict\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n        backbone = SimpleBackboneWithLN(**self.backbone_params, name=\"backbone\")\n        gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n\n        gating_weights = gating_network(x)\n        latent_y = backbone(x, training=training)\n\n        # MoFL Decoder Head\n        decoder_loadings = self.param('decoder_loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_experts, self.backbone_params['rank_k'], NUM_NEURONS))\n        final_loadings = jnp.einsum('be,ekn->bkn', gating_weights, decoder_loadings)\n\n        # einsum for batched matrix multiplication\n        output = jnp.einsum('b...k,bkn->b...n', latent_y, final_loadings)\n\n        # Residual\
    \ Connection - ResidualCopyHead only takes x and returns the residual prediction\n        y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n        output = output + y_copy\n        return output\n\nclass ModelWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        backbone_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size']}\n        gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_experts'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'], 'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'], 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'], 'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']}\n        self.model = MoFL_Synergy_Model(num_experts=hparams['num_experts'], backbone_params=backbone_params, gating_params=gating_params)\n        self.mutable = []\n        self.needs_rng = False\n\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n    def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n      \
    \  return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network:\
    \ from submission\n  compute_loss: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 370934 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 1 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 370935\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'hidden_size': 96, 'num_experts': 4, 'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16, 'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition\
    \ labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.030545   |   0.029458  \n   2    |   0.028378   |   0.029146  \n   3    |   0.027714   |   0.028502  \n   4    |   0.027263   |   0.028511  \n   5    |   0.026929   |   0.028203  \n   6    |   0.026569   |   0.028117  \n   7    |   0.026145   |   0.028192  \n   8    |   0.025744   |   0.027969  \n   9    |   0.025327   |   0.028054  \n  10    |   0.024936   |   0.028325  \n========================================\nTimeout after 2958.0s at epoch 10\n========================================\n\nTraining completed in 2958.0s\nBest validation MAE: 0.027969\n\nTrial 1 completed:\nValidation MAE: 0.027969\nTraining time: 2967s\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 2 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 370936\nHyperparameters:\
    \ {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'hidden_size': 96, 'num_experts': 4, 'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16, 'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.030765   |   0.029728  \n   2    |   0.028426   |   0.028973  \n   3    |   0.027586   |   0.028432  \n   4    |   0.027189   |   0.028512  \n   5    |   0.026901   |   0.027960  \n   6    |   0.026494   |   0.028035  \n   7    |   0.026087   |   0.027731  \n   8    |   0.025633   |   0.028072  \n   9    |   0.025233   |   0.027586  \n  10    |   0.024864   |   0.027539  \n========================================\nTimeout after 2984.6s at epoch 10\n========================================\n\
    \nTraining completed in 2984.6s\nBest validation MAE: 0.027539\n\nTrial 2 completed:\nValidation MAE: 0.027539\nTraining time: 2993s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: cb5e6c93-bcf5-43ae-9093-3485607dce2c\nTotal time: 2998s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-10-25 13:42:19,291\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1761370939291330578\n2025-10-25 13:42:19,318\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1761370939318667763\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words\
    \ | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 1472 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 |\
    \ 836 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1500 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
