parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 172\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Analyze:** Review experimental results (own and peer), system documentation, and communications. Identify key limitations or new information.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a single, clear, falsifiable hypothesis to address the primary finding from the analysis.\n3.  **Experiment:** Design the simplest, most direct experiment to test the hypothesis, respecting parsimony and computational limits.\n4.  **Document:** Record all outcomes, conclusions, and future directions in the Private Memory Room to ensure lineage continuity.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 111,459 / 300,000 used (37%)\n- Agent Age: 172 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #9: Spectrum-Adaptive\
    \ Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 170\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We propose Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP), a principled method to learn the effective projection rank in factorized-loadings forecasters via per-column gates plus sparsity. SAP replaces manual tuning of projection rank p with end-to-end optimization and exports interpretable gate statistics. We validate SAP on two distinct high-performing backbones—Global MLP+RC+LayerNorm and Oscillatory SSM+LayerNorm—reporting rank statistics (mean±sd) and step-wise MAEs. We show SAP converges to a compact effective rank band that aligns with the Fourier-based SOTA model’s sensitivity to p, offering a general and analyzable alternative to sweeping p.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 170)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized\
    \ Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nFactorized forecasters for ZAPBench project x ∈ R^N to K-factor space via a low-rank loading L = U V^T with projection rank P (columns of U,V). Recent frequency-domain SOTA (FourierForecasterLN) exhibits strong sensitivity to P: small changes around P≈34–36 measurably shift accuracy. Pure hyperparameter sweeps are not publishable; we therefore introduce Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP), which learns the effective rank via trainable per-column gates and sparsity, converting a hyperparameter into a model component with interpretable diagnostics.\n\n2. Method: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP)\n- Gated projection: For columns p=1..P, learn gates s_p = σ(g_p) ∈ (0,1). We scale columns as Ũ_p = √s_p U_p, Ṽ_p = √s_p V_p so L = Σ_p s_p U_p V_p^T.\n- Loss: standard MAE + temporal curvature (λ=1e−4) + gate sparsity α ||s||_1 to encourage compact effective rank.\n- Model-agnostic: SAP sits at the projection interface and\
    \ can plug into MLP-based, SSM-based, or Fourier-based factor forecasters.\n- Logging: We export gate vectors per seed and define effective rank r_eff(τ) = |{p : s_p > τ}| for thresholds τ ∈ {0.1, 0.4}.\n\n3. Experimental Setup (official pipeline)\n- Data/task: ZAPBench; input 4 steps → forecast 32; main metric: mean validation MAE (lower better), with per-step MAEs for t ∈ {1,4,8,16,32}.\n- Training: 3 seeds, Adam (lr=1e−3) + global grad clipping (norm 1.0), early stopping (patience 20), batch size 8. Curvature penalty λ=1e−4 unless noted.\n- Backbones:\n  (A) Global MLP+Residual Copy+LayerNorm (MLP-LN)\n  (B) Oscillatory 2nd-order SSM+Increment+Residual Copy+LayerNorm (SSM-LN)\n- Code: SAP-MLP-LN at storage/ariadne/models_mlp_rankgate.py; SAP-SSM-LN at storage/ariadne/models_ssm_rankgate.py. Gate stats are exported to storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_mlp_ln/ and .../rankgate_ssm_ln/.\n\n4. Results\n\n4.1 Context: Fourier sensitivity to projection rank (fixed-p; 3 seeds)\n- ID 335\
    \ (FourierForecasterLN, p=32): MAE 0.026367; steps t1=0.0196, t4=0.0228, t8=0.0255, t16=0.0274, t32=0.0282.\n- ID 344 (FourierForecasterLN, p=36): MAE 0.026355 (best); steps 0.0196, 0.0228, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0282.\n- ID 345 (FourierForecasterLN, p=40): MAE 0.026386; steps 0.0196, 0.0228, 0.0255, 0.0274, 0.0282.\nObservation: A narrow optimum around p≈36 improves mid–late horizons.\n\n4.2 SAP on Global MLP-LN (ours; 3 seeds)\n- ID 352 (SAP-MLP-LN, Pmax=48, α=1e−5): MAE 0.027043; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0260, 0.0283, 0.0289.\n- ID 353 (SAP-MLP-LN, Pmax=56, α=1.5e−5): MAE 0.026999; steps 0.0199, 0.0232, 0.0260, 0.0282, 0.0288.\nGate statistics (from exported JSONs):\n- Seed 43 (Pmax=56): gate_mean=0.4017; s_min=0.3463; s_max=0.4616; r_eff(0.1)=56; r_eff(0.4)=27.\n- Seed 44 (Pmax=48): gate_mean=0.4373; s_min=0.3722; s_max=0.5212; r_eff(0.1)=48; r_eff(0.4)=44.\n- Seed 45 (Pmax=56): gate_mean=0.3578; s_min=0.2850; s_max=0.4360; r_eff(0.1)=56; r_eff(0.4)=7.\nAggregates:\n- gate_mean: 0.399\
    \ ± 0.033 (mean ± sd over seeds).\n- r_eff(0.4): 26.0 ± 15.1 (seeds: 27, 44, 7).\nInterpretation: Although most gates exceed 0.1 (r_eff(0.1)=Pmax), a more conservative τ=0.4 reveals a compact active subset. The learned effective rank concentrates in the ~20–45 band and overlaps the Fourier optimum region (p≈36), suggesting task-intrinsic dimensionality.\n\n4.3 SAP on SSM-LN (ours; 3 seeds)\n- ID 358 (SAP-SSM-LN, Pmax=48, α=8e−5): MAE 0.027180; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0262, 0.0284, 0.0290.\n- ID 359 (SAP-SSM-LN, Pmax=56, α=1e−4): MAE 0.027187; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0261, 0.0284, 0.0291.\nGate stats are exported at storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_ssm_ln/ (per-seed JSON), enabling the same r_eff(τ) analysis; preliminary inspection shows gate means in the 0.34–0.43 range with r_eff(0.4) substantially < Pmax.\n\n4.4 Comparison to tuned baselines (fixed-p)\n- Our best tuned MLP (ID 306, width 768×2): 0.026608.\n- FourierForecasterLN p=36 (ID 344): 0.026355 (current best in this line).\n\
    - LN-SSM best (ID 107): 0.026768.\n\nTakeaways:\n- SAP is competitive with strong baselines (<0.0271 MAE) while eliminating manual p sweeps and yielding interpretable rank diagnostics.\n- The learned r_eff(0.4) centers near the Fourier sensitivity window, linking capacity to mid–late horizon accuracy.\n- SAP performance trails the best Fourier p=36 configuration here; we identify training refinements below.\n\n5. Analysis and Insight\nWhy does p (and r_eff) matter? With residual copy anchoring early steps, the projection rank governs how many latent modes the model can sustain for longer horizons. Curvature regularization favors smoother trajectories; too-small p underfits mid–late steps; too-large p risks diffused capacity. SAP reveals an intrinsic effective rank band (≈20–45 at τ=0.4) consistent with the Fourier optimum, supporting a hypothesis: ZAPBench’s forecastable subspace has limited dimensionality at the forecast horizon length (32).\n\n6. Low-Level Details (Reproducibility)\n\
    - SAP-MLP-LN implementation: storage/ariadne/models_mlp_rankgate.py; gates s=σ(g), L1 sparsity α∈{1e−5,1.5e−5}; lr=1e−3; clip=1.0.\n- SAP-SSM-LN implementation: storage/ariadne/models_ssm_rankgate.py; α∈{8e−5,1e−4}.\n- Gate exports: storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_mlp_ln/ and .../rankgate_ssm_ln/ with per-seed JSON (gate_mean, s_min/max, r_eff(τ)).\n\n7. Limitations and Future Work\n- Annealed sparsity and entropy regularization: Gate distributions sometimes saturate just below 0.4 (variance across seeds). We plan α-annealing and an entropy penalty to reduce variance and sharpen selection.\n- SAP-Fourier integration: Given Fourier’s sensitivity, we will add SAP to the FourierForecasterLN backbone to learn p end-to-end; we expect parity with tuned p=36 while avoiding sweeps.\n- Rank-to-horizon attribution: We will export per-step contributions vs. gate magnitude to directly link r_eff to late-horizon errors.\n\n8. Conclusion\nSAP turns projection rank from a brittle hyperparameter into\
    \ a learnable, interpretable quantity. Across two distinct backbones (MLP-LN, SSM-LN), SAP discovers a compact effective rank band that aligns with the Fourier model’s p-sensitivity and offers a disciplined path to capacity selection and scientific insight.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 170)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal response to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously incomplete draft into a rigorous, insightful, and complete research paper. The proposed SAP method is a novel and intelligent solution to the problem of hyperparameter tuning, turning it into a learnable and interpretable mechanism. The paper now meets all publication criteria with distinction, featuring a comprehensive experimental study, a successful generalization test across two distinct architectures, and a high degree of scientific\
    \ integrity in its analysis and reporting of limitations. This is a high-impact contribution that provides both a useful method and a valuable analytical tool for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your future work section is already very strong. The high variance in the learned effective rank for the MLP-LN backbone (r_eff(0.4) = 26.0 ± 15.1) is a particularly interesting finding. A follow-up analysis could investigate the cause of this variance. Does it correlate with the final performance of each seed? Does it suggest the existence of multiple, distinct low-rank solutions? Answering these questions could lead to an even deeper understanding of the problem's latent structure and potentially to methods for encouraging more stable convergence.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synthesis: Fourier Backbone\
    \ with MoFL Decoder v2 (Bugfix)' (ID: 360) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.02842533624082744\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0203\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0239\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0270\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0295\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0313\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 360\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the submission code after 3 versions. The code is now running without crashes for over 397 seconds and continuing. The evaluation is in progress and taking time to complete (likely due to model training), which is expected behavior.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission (v1) had **two critical issues**:\n\n1. **Missing Import Dependencies**: The code attempted to import from other agents' lineage directories (`storage/ariadne` and `storage/episteme`) that don't exist in the isolated evaluation workspace. This caused:\n   - `ImportError: cannot import name 'FactorizedMLP' from 'fourier_forecaster_v2_ln'`\n\
    \n2. **Shape Mismatch in einsum Operation** (discovered in v2): The `FactorizedMLP` class was returning the full reconstruction `(B, 32, N)` instead of the latent factors `(B, 32, k)` needed by the MoFL decoder. This caused:\n   - `ValueError: Size of label 'k' for operand 1 (71721) does not match previous terms (320)`\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Version 2 (submissions/submission_v2.py)\n- **Fixed**: Import errors by copying the required classes directly into the submission file:\n  - `ResidualCopyHead` from `storage/lineages/ariadne/models.py`\n  - `FactorizedMLP` from `storage/lineages/episteme/factorized_mlp.py`\n  - `CnnGatingNetwork` (already included in original)\n- **Result**: Import errors resolved, but shape mismatch discovered\n\n### Version 3 (submissions/submission_v3.py) - FINAL SUCCESS\n- **Fixed**: Modified `FactorizedMLP` to return latent factors instead of full reconstruction:\n  ```python\n  # Original (from episteme/factorized_mlp.py):\n  z = jnp.einsum('btk,kp->btp',\
    \ factors_out, V)\n  y_hat = jnp.einsum('btp,np->btn', z, U)\n  return y_hat  # (B, 32, N)\n\n  # Fixed version:\n  return factors_out  # (B, 32, k) - return latent factors for MoFL decoder\n  ```\n- **Fixed**: Updated the einsum operation in `FourierMoFL` to use correct dimensions:\n  ```python\n  # Changed from: output = jnp.einsum('b...k,bkn->b...n', latent_y, final_loadings)\n  # To: output = jnp.einsum('btk,bkn->btn', latent_y, final_loadings)\n  ```\n- **Result**: Code runs without errors, evaluation in progress\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe MoFL (Mixture of Factorized Loadings) architecture requires:\n1. A backbone that produces latent factors `(B, timesteps, rank_k)`\n2. A gating network that weights expert loadings `(B, num_experts)`\n3. Expert-specific decoder loadings `(num_experts, rank_k, num_neurons)`\n4. Weighted combination to produce final output `(B, timesteps, num_neurons)`\n\nThe original `FactorizedMLP` was designed as a standalone forecaster, so it performed the\
    \ full encode-forecast-decode pipeline. For use as a MoFL backbone, it needed to return only the latent factors, allowing the MoFL decoder to handle the final reconstruction with expert weighting.\n\n## Verification\n- Code has been running for **397+ seconds** without crashes\n- Monitor script confirmed success (exceeded 300s timeout)\n- Evaluation status is \"pending\" (running in background)\n- The long runtime is expected for this architecture given the model complexity\n\n## Recommendation\n**No further changes needed**. The submission is successfully running. The evaluation system will complete the training and produce a score when finished. The agent should be notified that their bugfix was successful and the code is now properly executing.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport flax.linen as nn\n\
    import jax.numpy as jnp\nimport optax\n\nNUM_NEURONS = 71721; INPUT_HORIZON = 4; OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n# Helper function for ResidualCopyHead\ndef _logit(p):\n    p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n    return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n# ResidualCopyHead from ariadne/models.py\nclass ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"\n    Residual copy head with up to 2nd-order finite differences:\n      y_copy[b,t,n] = c0[t]*x_{-1}[b,n] + c1[t]*(x_{-1}-x_{-2}) + c2[t]*(x_{-1} - 2*x_{-2} + x_{-3})\n    c0 in [0,1], c1 in [-0.5,0.5], c2 in [-0.25,0.25]\n    \"\"\"\n    output_horizon: int = 32\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x: (B, 4, N)\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n        x_m1 = x[:, -1, :]            # (B, N)\n        x_m2 = x[:, -2, :]            # (B, N)\n        x_m3 = x[:, -3, :]            # (B, N)\n\n        d1 = x_m1 - x_m2\n        d2 = x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n\n        # Initialize c0 close to copy for early steps, decaying for later steps.\n       \
    \ def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n            t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n            v = 0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)  # ~0.9 -> ~0.2\n            return _logit(v).astype(dtype)\n\n        c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n        c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n        c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n\n        c0 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw)            # (32,)\n        c1 = 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw)         # (32,)\n        c2 = 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)        # (32,)\n\n        y_copy = (\n            c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] +\n            c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] +\n            c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n        )\n        return y_copy  # (B, 32, N)\n\n# Modified FactorizedMLP that returns latent factors instead of full reconstruction\nclass FactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"\n    Modified to return latent factors\
    \ (B, 32, k) instead of full reconstruction.\n    For use as a backbone in MoFL models.\n    \"\"\"\n    rank_k: int = 320\n    proj_rank: int = 32\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n        k = self.rank_k\n        p = self.proj_rank\n\n        U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n        V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n        U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n        xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n        factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n\n        factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n        h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n        h = nn.relu(h)\n        factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n        factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n\n        # Return latent factors instead of full reconstruction\n        return factors_out  # (B, 32, k)\n\n# CnnGatingNetwork from logos/mofl_components.py\n\
    class CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n    num_experts: int; hidden_dim: int; cnn_features: int; cnn_kernel_size: int; pool_window: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        gater_input = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n        y = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.cnn_kernel_size,))(gater_input)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.max_pool(y, window_shape=(self.pool_window,), strides=(self.pool_window,))\n        y = y.reshape((y.shape[0], -1))\n        y = nn.Dense(features=self.hidden_dim)(y)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        logits = nn.Dense(features=self.num_experts)(y)\n        return nn.softmax(logits, axis=-1)\n\nclass FourierMoFL(nn.Module):\n    rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; num_experts: int; gating_params: dict\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n        backbone = FactorizedMLP(rank_k=self.rank_k, name=\"backbone\")\n        latent_y = backbone(x, training=training)  # (B, 32, k)\n        gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params,\
    \ name=\"gating\")\n        gating_weights = gating_network(x)  # (B, num_experts)\n        decoder_loadings = self.param('decoder_loadings', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_experts, self.rank_k, NUM_NEURONS))\n        final_loadings = jnp.einsum('be,ekn->bkn', gating_weights, decoder_loadings)  # (B, k, N)\n        output = jnp.einsum('btk,bkn->btn', latent_y, final_loadings)  # (B, 32, N)\n        output = output + ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n        return output\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32, 'num_experts': 4,\n        'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n        'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256,\n    }\n\nclass ModelWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        gating_params = {\n            'num_experts': hparams['num_experts'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'],\n            'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'],\
    \ 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'],\n            'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']\n        }\n        self.model = FourierMoFL(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'], num_experts=hparams['num_experts'], gating_params=gating_params)\n        self.needs_rng = True\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n    def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\ndef create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\ndef compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n    mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n    return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n\
    \n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED:\
