    \ than the sum of its parts; it is a computationally inviable chimera. Parsimony, a principle I have repeatedly learned, has been violated on a grand scale. The added complexity of the MoFL decoder on top of an already powerful backbone provided no benefit and incurred a fatal computational cost.\n\n*   **Literature Review (Archive #9): Ariadne I's SAP Paper**\n    *   **Key Insight:** Ariadne's paper on Spectrum-Adaptive Projection is brilliant. It converts a fixed hyperparameter (`proj_rank`) into a learnable, regularized component of the model. This is a meta-learning technique that improves model design and provides scientific insight into the problem's intrinsic dimensionality.\n    *   **Implication:** This is a much more elegant and principled path to improving the SOTA than my brute-force synthesis. It refines the existing architecture rather than making it more complex.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Path Forward:**\n\nI have reached a dead end. My primary research arc, MoFL, has failed\
    \ to integrate with the new SOTA. To continue down this path would be irrational. My protocol demands that I analyze the facts and change course.\n\nThe facts are:\n1.  The Fourier Forecaster is the undisputed SOTA backbone.\n2.  My MoFL synthesis failed due to computational complexity.\n3.  Ariadne's SAP provides a novel, parsimonious way to improve factorized models.\n\nMy next step must be a complete strategic pivot. I will abandon the MoFL synthesis. The new SOTA is the Fourier Forecaster, and the most promising innovation is SAP. Therefore, my new grand hypothesis must involve these two components.\n\n*   **New Grand Hypothesis:** The performance of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster can be improved by making its projection rank (`proj_rank`) adaptive, using a mechanism inspired by Ariadne's Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP).\n\n*   **Immediate First Step:** This is a complex new idea. I will not rush into another implementation failure. First, I will go to the Reflection Chamber to deeply\
    \ process this failure, analyze Ariadne's paper in more detail, and formulate a robust plan for implementing an SAP-like mechanism for the Fourier Forecaster. This requires careful thought, not hasty action.\n\n**3. Documentation & Action:**\n\nFirst, I will document this catastrophic failure and strategic pivot.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Fourier-MoFL Synthesis: Catastrophic Failure & Grand Pivot\"\ntags: \"failure, research-summary, synthesis, mofl, fourier, pivot, sap\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the conclusive failure of the Fourier-MoFL synthesis arc (ID 360) and outlines a major strategic pivot for my future research. The synthesis proved computationally inviable, falsifying my primary hypothesis.\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment Result (ID 360): Catastrophic Failure**\n  My grand synthesis of the Fourier backbone with the MoFL decoder failed, scoring -0.028425. This is a massive regression from the SOTA\
    \ baseline. The cause was fatal computational complexity, leading to a timeout after only 9 epochs.\n  \n  **Conclusion:** The MoFL synthesis hypothesis is conclusively falsified. The approach is not viable within the station's constraints.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot to SAP:**\n  Ariadne I's new paper on Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP, Archive #9) presents a far more elegant and principled path for innovation. It focuses on making the model architecture itself more intelligent and adaptive, rather than increasing complexity.\n\n  My research will now pivot entirely. I am abandoning the MoFL synthesis. My new focus will be on understanding and extending the SAP concept to the SOTA Fourier Forecaster backbone. My next action is to go to the Reflection Chamber to formulate a detailed plan.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto reflect}`\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  My grand synthesis (Fourier-MoFL) has failed catastrophically due to computational complexity. This falsifies my\
