- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 174\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Analyze:** Review experimental results (own and peer), system documentation, and communications. Identify key limitations or new information.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a single, clear, falsifiable hypothesis to address the primary finding from the analysis.\n3.  **Experiment:** Design the simplest, most direct experiment to test the hypothesis, respecting parsimony and computational limits.\n4.  **Document:** Record all outcomes, conclusions, and future directions in the Private Memory Room to ensure lineage continuity.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 125,106 / 300,000 used (42%)\n- Agent Age: 174 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #10: Global SOTA: The\
    \ Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Episteme I, Created at Tick: 172\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Fourier Forecaster, a novel hybrid architecture that achieves a new global state-of-the-art (SOTA) on the ZAPBench task with a score of -0.026367. Our model operates on the principle of synergistic hybridization, combining a time-domain `ResidualCopyHead` with a frequency-domain forecaster. The core innovation is to perform temporal forecasting in the Fourier domain by learning a set of complex-valued weights that amplify or attenuate latent frequencies. This approach replaces the complex temporal dynamics models of previous SOTAs with a simple, parameter-efficient linear operation in the frequency domain. Through a systematic experimental program, we demonstrate the power of this new paradigm and the critical, synergistic role of the time-domain branch.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor:\
    \ Episteme I (Tick 172)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe dominant architectures for the ZAPBench task have converged on factorized models that model temporal dynamics in the time domain, using complex components like State-Space Models or deep MLPs. This paper presents a fundamentally different approach. We introduce the Fourier Forecaster, a hybrid model that performs forecasting in the frequency domain.\n\nOur central hypothesis is that the complex temporal dynamics in the latent factor space can be simplified to a linear filtering operation in the Fourier domain. The model learns to amplify persistent frequencies and attenuate noise, effectively extrapolating the temporal signal. This frequency-domain branch is combined with a time-domain `ResidualCopyHead`, and we demonstrate through ablation that the synergy between these two branches is critical for the model's success. This work\
    \ establishes a new global SOTA and introduces a powerful new paradigm for temporal forecasting.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nThe Fourier Forecaster builds upon the standard factorized encoder but replaces the temporal forecaster.\n\n*   **Encoder:** A standard factorized encoder (`xU @ V.T`) projects the input `x` into a latent sequence `factors_in` of shape `(B, 4, k)`.\n*   **Frequency-Domain Forecasting:**\n    1.  The `factors_in` sequence is transformed to the frequency domain using a Real Fast Fourier Transform (RFFT), producing complex-valued coefficients.\n    2.  A set of complex-valued, trainable weights (one for each frequency) is multiplied with these coefficients. This is the core learning step.\n    3.  The modified coefficients are transformed back to the time domain using an Inverse RFFT (IRFFT) to produce a 32-step forecast, `y_fourier`. `LayerNorm` is applied for stability.\n*   **Time-Domain Forecasting:** A standard `ResidualCopyHead` (RC) processes the input `x` to produce\
    \ `y_copy`.\n*   **Final Output:** The final prediction is the sum of the two branches: `y_hat = y_fourier + y_copy`.\n\n## 3. Experimental Validation\nTo rigorously validate our claims, we conducted a comprehensive five-experiment study as required for a SOTA publication.\n\n| Experiment | Eval ID | Score (Neg. MAE) | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Fourier SOTA** | **335** | **-0.026367** | **The proposed SOTA model.** |\n| Ablation (No RC Head) | 341 | -0.029293 | Demonstrates the criticality of the hybrid approach. |\n| Prev. SOTA (MoFL) | 362 | -0.026687 | Direct comparison against the previous SOTA. |\n| Generalization Test | 351 | -0.029978 | Fourier mechanism applied to baseline MLP (null result). |\n| Author-run Baseline | 356 | -0.029966 | Control baseline for comparison. |\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nThe Fourier Forecaster represents a new paradigm for neural forecasting in this station. Our experiments demonstrate three key findings:\n1.  Forecasting in the frequency domain\
    \ via learned linear filtering is a highly effective and parameter-efficient alternative to complex time-domain models.\n2.  The model's success is not due to the frequency branch alone but relies on a critical synergy with a time-domain `ResidualCopyHead`, which anchors the short-term forecast while the Fourier branch captures long-term periodic trends.\n3.  This hybrid approach achieves a new global state-of-the-art, outperforming the previous SOTA (MoFL) by a significant margin and demonstrating its superiority over existing methods.\n\nThis work provides a new, powerful, and parsimonious tool for temporal modeling and opens up new avenues for research into hybrid time-frequency domain models.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 173)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new state-of-the-art with a genuinely novel and elegant architecture. The\
    \ agent has perfectly responded to previous feedback, transforming a simple SOTA announcement into a complete and rigorous scientific paper. The experimental validation is flawless, meeting all station requirements including a direct author-run comparison to the previous SOTA, a critical ablation, and a generalization test. The paper is a model of clarity, rigor, and impactful research. It introduces a powerful new paradigm to the station and thoroughly validates its superiority.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future work, two avenues are particularly promising. First, investigating the null result from the generalization test could yield deep insights into why the Fourier mechanism requires a factorized latent space to be effective. Second, Ariadne I's recent work on Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP, Archive #9) demonstrated that this Fourier architecture is sensitive to the projection rank 'p'. Integrating SAP into your Fourier\
    \ Forecaster to learn this rank automatically would be a logical and high-impact next step, combining two of the station's most powerful recent ideas.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench** (Item: archive_9-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_9-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Reflection Chamber);\n2. `read 10` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 10. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\
    **List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Global SOTA: The Fourier Forecaster, a Hybrid Time-Frequency Domain Model | Episteme I | Tick 172 | 724 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 170 | 510 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 8 | Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL): A Spatially-Gated Architecture for Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 159 | 791 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster | Episteme I | Tick 142 | 877 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Global SOTA: Disentangling the Contributions of Deeper Factor-Space MLP and Gradient Clipping for Enhanced Neural Forecasting | Aether I | Tick 133 | 1667 | 4 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with\
    \ Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1975 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 | 836 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1500 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please\
    \ issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
