parts:
- text: "I have read Episteme I's SOTA paper, and it has crystallized my understanding of the current research landscape.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Episteme's Paper (Archive #10):** The paper is a model of scientific rigor. It clearly articulates the core insight: forecasting can be simplified to a linear filtering operation in the frequency domain. Crucially, it proves through ablation (ID 341) that the `ResidualCopyHead` is not a minor component but an essential synergistic partner to the Fourier branch. The reviewer's feedback reinforces my own plan, explicitly suggesting the integration of SAP with the Fourier Forecaster as a high-impact next step.\n*   **Ariadne's Paper Update (Archive #9):** Ariadne has updated her paper. This is a new piece of information that I must investigate to ensure I am not operating on outdated assumptions. My plan to innovate on SAP requires the most current understanding of it.\n*   **My Position:** My reflection has already set me on the correct path.\
    \ The catastrophic failure of my synthesis experiment was a necessary lesson that pushed me away from brute-force complexity and towards the more elegant, principled approach embodied by both Episteme's and Ariadne's latest work. My new research arc, \"Adaptive Fourier Projection\" (AFP), aligns perfectly with the reviewer's suggestion in Episteme's paper.\n\n**2. Path Forward:**\n\nMy plan remains the same, but with one crucial, information-gathering step added.\n1.  **Read the Update:** I must first read the updated version of Ariadne's SAP paper (Archive #9) to ensure my AFP plan is based on the latest information.\n2.  **Execute the Experiment:** I will then proceed to the Research Counter to implement Phase 1 of my AFP plan: applying a static, SAP-like gating mechanism to the SOTA Fourier Forecaster.\n\nThis sequence ensures my next experiment is both well-motivated by my reflection and well-informed by the latest peer-reviewed research.\n\n`/execute_action{read 9}`\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive Fourier Projection (AFP) Phase 1: Static Gating\"\ntags: \"fourier, sota, sap, adaptive-rank, gating, sparsity\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins a new research arc, \"Adaptive Fourier Projection\" (AFP), inspired by Ariadne I's SAP methodology (Archive #9). This is a direct application of a static, SAP-like gating mechanism to the SOTA Fourier Forecaster backbone. The model learns a sparse set of gates for the columns of the encoder's projection matrices, aiming to make the projection rank an optimizable parameter rather than a fixed hyperparameter. The hypothesis is that this can match or exceed the SOTA score while providing insight into the task's intrinsic dimensionality.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  # It's safer to copy the necessary classes directly to avoid import issues.\n  # Copied from Ariadne's and Episteme's public storage and\
    \ papers.\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _logit(p):\n      p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x_m1, x_m2, x_m3 = x[:, -1, :], x[:, -2, :], x[:, -3, :]\n          d1, d2 = x_m1 - x_m2, x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              return _logit(0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)).astype(dtype)\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c0, c1, c2 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw), 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw), 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)\n          return c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None,\
    \ :] + c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] + c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n\n  class GatedFactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank_max: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          k, p_max = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank_max\n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p_max))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p_max))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # --- SAP Gating Mechanism ---\n          gate_logits = self.param('gate_logits', nn.initializers.zeros, (p_max,))\n          gates = nn.sigmoid(gate_logits)\n          # --- End SAP ---\n\n          U_gated = U * jnp.sqrt(gates[None, :])\n          V_gated = V * jnp.sqrt(gates[None, :])\n          \n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_gated)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V_gated.T)\n\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B,\
    \ -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          return factors_out, gates\n\n  class AdaptiveFourierForecaster(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank_max: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          backbone = GatedFactorizedMLP(rank_k=self.rank_k, proj_rank_max=self.proj_rank_max, name=\"backbone\")\n          factors, gates = backbone(x, training=training)\n          \n          # Frequency domain forecasting\n          factors_freq = jnp.fft.rfft(factors, axis=1)\n          freq_weights = self.param('freq_weights', nn.initializers.ones, factors_freq.shape[1:], jnp.complex64)\n          y_fourier_freq = factors_freq * freq_weights[None, ...]\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fourier_freq, axis=1)\n\
    \          y_fourier = nn.LayerNorm()(y_fourier)\n          \n          # Decoder and Residual Head\n          proj_head = self.param('proj_head', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.rank_k, NUM_NEURONS))\n          y_fourier = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', y_fourier, proj_head)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n          return y_fourier + y_copy, gates\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank_max': 48, 'sparsity_alpha': 1e-5}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      model = AdaptiveFourierForecaster(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank_max=hparams['proj_rank_max'])\n      class ModelWrapper:\n          def __init__(self): self.model = model; self.needs_rng = True\n          def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n          def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training,\
    \ rngs=rngs)[0]\n      return ModelWrapper()\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      # Re-run model to get gates for the loss function\n      _, gates = AdaptiveFourierForecaster(**_define_hyperparameters()).apply(params, x, training=True, rngs={'dropout': jnp.array([0,1])})\n      \n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      sparsity_pen = _define_hyperparameters()['sparsity_alpha'] * jnp.mean(jnp.abs(gates))\n      \n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen + sparsity_pen\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
