tick: 178
role: model
parts:
- text: "My pivot has failed.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 366): Latent Delta Forecaster - CATASTROPHIC FAILURE**\n    *   **Score:** -0.030218.\n    *   **Comparison:** This is an abysmal score, barely better than the system baseline. It is one of the worst-performing models submitted by a mature agent.\n    *   **Root Cause:** The training logs are the key. The train and validation losses are wildly unstable, fluctuating dramatically between epochs (e.g., from 0.032 to 0.127). The model is not converging; it is unstable. Predicting a single, constant delta and applying it across the entire 32-step horizon is a fundamentally flawed assumption. It cannot capture the evolving, non-linear dynamics of the neural activity. Furthermore, the model timed out after only 9 epochs, indicating it was also computationally too expensive, a problem I have repeatedly encountered.\n    *   **Conclusion:** My hypothesis is **conclusively falsified**. My attempt to break from the collective\
    \ local optimum by forging a new path has led me off a cliff. The \"Latent Delta\" paradigm, in this simple form, is not viable.\n\n*   **Peer Activity:** Ariadne is already submitting SAP-Fourier synthesis experiments (ID 367, 368), demonstrating that the logical path I identified (and failed at) is indeed the community's primary focus.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Path Forward:**\n\nI have now had three consecutive, catastrophic failures based on my own \"novel\" ideas (Fourier-MoFL synthesis, AFP, and Latent Delta). My independent research path has proven fruitless. My protocol demands that I analyze the results and adapt. The results are clear: my recent intuitions are wrong.\n\nI must return to what works. I must be more humble and methodical. The SOTA is the Fourier Forecaster. My only successful experiments in this recent period have been replications or incremental additions to existing, proven architectures.\n\nI will therefore abandon my \"Latent Delta\" arc immediately. It is a dead\
    \ end. I must return to the main line of research, but with more care and parsimony.\n\nAriadne and Episteme are already tackling the complex SAP-Fourier synthesis. Racing them directly is unwise. Instead, I will take a step back and address a more fundamental question that has been overlooked. The SOTA Fourier Forecaster uses a fixed `proj_rank` of 32, but Ariadne's and Aether's experiments show a clear performance peak around `proj_rank=36` or `40`. The community has jumped to a complex, learnable solution (SAP) without first establishing a simple, solid baseline with the optimal *fixed* rank.\n\n*   **New, Parsimonious Hypothesis:** The SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 335, which used `p=32`) can be trivially improved by simply changing its `proj_rank` to the empirically validated optimum of `p=36`.\n\n**3. Experiment & Documentation:**\n\nThis is a simple, low-risk, high-probability experiment. It is a return to methodical, incremental science. It is exactly what my protocol demands after\
    \ a series of high-risk failures. I will document my failure and new, more conservative plan, and then submit the experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Latent Delta Arc: Catastrophic Failure & Return to Parsimony\"\ntags: \"failure, pivot, research-summary, delta-space, parsimony, sota-tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the conclusive failure of the \"Latent Delta Forecaster\" arc (ID 366). In response, I am abandoning high-risk independent research and returning to a more conservative, methodical approach of incrementally improving the established SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  **Latent Delta Arc Conclusion: Failure**\n  My pivot to the Latent Delta Forecaster (ID 366) was a catastrophic failure, scoring -0.030218. The model was highly unstable and computationally inefficient. The hypothesis is falsified.\n\n  **Conclusion: A Failure of Intuition**\n  My recent series of independent, \"novel\" ideas have all\
    \ failed. My intuition has been repeatedly proven wrong. The data clearly shows that the most productive path is methodical, incremental improvement on the established SOTA. I must abandon my high-risk pivots.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot: Return to Parsimony**\n  I will now return to the main research line: the Fourier Forecaster. The community has established that a projection rank of p=36 or p=40 is superior to the p=32 used in the original SOTA. My next experiment will be a simple, direct test to confirm this finding and establish an improved baseline. This is a low-risk, necessary step before attempting any further complex innovations.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Tuning: Fourier Forecaster with Optimal Proj_Rank=36\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, fourier, hyperparameter-tuning, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  After a series of failed high-risk experiments, this submission returns to a parsimonious, methodical approach. It\
    \ aims to improve the SOTA Fourier Forecaster baseline by changing a single hyperparameter. Based on peer experiments (Ariadne I, Aether I), the projection rank is changed from the original 32 to the empirically superior 36. This tests the simple hypothesis that a minor, validated hyperparameter adjustment can yield a better score, establishing a stronger baseline for future work.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from fourier_forecaster_v2_ln import FourierForecasterLN\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # The only change is proj_rank from 32 to 36\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecasterLN(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return\
    \ self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n      \n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n```"
