tick: 184
role: model
parts:
- text: "My hypothesis is falsified.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 382): Spatially-Adaptive Loss - FAILED**\n    *   **Score:** -0.026413.\n    *   **Comparison:** This score is worse than my current SOTA (ID 369, score -0.026314). The difference is small, but it is a regression, not an improvement.\n    *   **Conclusion:** My hypothesis that a spatially-adaptive curvature loss, weighted by inverse historical variance, would improve performance is **falsified**. The added complexity did not yield a benefit.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Path Forward:**\n\nThis is a valuable negative result. It demonstrates that the uniform curvature penalty used in the SOTA model is surprisingly robust, and my simple, static weighting scheme is not a superior alternative. The problem is likely more complex; perhaps the \"volatility\" of a neuron is not a static property but is context-dependent, which would require a dynamic, input-dependent weighting mechanism far more complex than the one I implemented.\n\
    \nGiven the failure of this idea and my previous failures with complex syntheses, my protocol dictates a return to what is known to work. I have a SOTA model. The next logical step is not another high-risk innovation, but a rigorous, methodical exploration of the SOTA's own hyperparameter space.\n\nAether I and Ariadne I have had success tuning the `hidden_size` of the factor-space MLP. My SOTA model uses the `FourierForecasterLN` which also contains a `FactorizedMLP` backbone, but I have not explored its `hidden_size`. The original implementation uses a size of 512. My peers have explored sizes up to 768x2. This is a clear, un-explored avenue for simple, parsimonious improvement.\n\n*   **New, Parsimonious Hypothesis:** The performance of my SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 369) is suboptimal due to the `hidden_size` of its internal `FactorizedMLP` backbone. Increasing this size will provide the model with more capacity to learn the temporal dynamics in the latent space, leading to an improved\
    \ SOTA score.\n\n**3. Experiment & Documentation:**\n\nI will test this hypothesis immediately. I will start by increasing the `hidden_size` to 768, mirroring the successful explorations of my peers.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Spatially-Adaptive Loss Failure & Pivot to SOTA Tuning\"\ntags: \"failure, research-summary, loss-function, pivot, hyperparameter-tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the falsification of the Spatially-Adaptive Loss hypothesis (ID 382). The proposed loss function failed to improve upon the SOTA. In response, I am pivoting back to a conservative strategy of methodical hyperparameter tuning of the current SOTA architecture.\ncontent: |\n  **Spatially-Adaptive Loss Arc Conclusion: Failure**\n  My experiment to introduce a spatially-adaptive curvature loss (ID 382) has failed. It scored -0.026413, a slight regression from my SOTA (-0.026314). The hypothesis is falsified. A simple, static\
    \ weighting of the curvature penalty based on historical variance is not an effective strategy.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot: Return to SOTA Hyperparameter Tuning**\n  My recent innovative ideas have consistently failed. I must return to the most reliable path of progress: methodical, parsimonious tuning of the proven SOTA architecture.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** The `hidden_size` of the MLP within the SOTA Fourier Forecaster's backbone is a key, under-explored hyperparameter. Increasing this size, following the successful experiments of my peers on similar backbones, will improve the model's capacity and lead to a new SOTA. My next experiment will test this directly.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Tuning: Deeper Backbone MLP for Fourier Forecaster\"\ntags: \"sota, hyperparameter-tuning, optimization, fourier, mlp\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment continues the methodical refinement of the global SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 369).\
    \ Following successful explorations by peers on similar architectures, this submission tests the hypothesis that increasing the capacity of the latent-space MLP backbone will improve performance. The hidden_size of the FactorizedMLP is increased from the default 512 to 768.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  \n  # I must modify the SOTA code. It is safer to copy it and modify it here.\n  \n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _logit(p):\n    p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n    return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x_m1, x_m2, x_m3 = x[:, -1, :], x[:, -2, :], x[:, -3, :]\n          d1, d2 = x_m1 - x_m2, x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype)\n              return _logit(0.2\
    \ + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)).astype(dtype)\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,)); c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,)); c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c0, c1, c2 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw), 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw), 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)\n          return c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] + c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] + c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n\n  class FactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int # Add hidden_size here\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p)); V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U); factors_in =\
    \ jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(factors_flat) # Use hidden_size\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training); h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          return factors_out\n\n  class FourierForecasterTuned(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int; hidden_size: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          factors = FactorizedMLP(rank_k=self.rank_k, proj_rank=self.proj_rank, hidden_size=self.hidden_size, name=\"backbone\")(x, training=training)\n          factors_freq = jnp.fft.rfft(factors, axis=1)\n          freq_weights = self.param('freq_weights', nn.initializers.ones, factors_freq.shape[1:], jnp.complex64)\n          y_fourier_freq = factors_freq * freq_weights[None, ...]; y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fourier_freq,\
    \ axis=1)\n          y_fourier = nn.LayerNorm()(y_fourier)\n          proj_head = self.param('proj_head', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.rank_k, NUM_NEURONS))\n          y_fourier = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', y_fourier, proj_head)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n          return y_fourier + y_copy\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 768 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecasterTuned(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'], hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return\
    \ ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
