tick: 188
role: model
parts:
- text: "My hypothesis is falsified. The MoFL paradigm appears to have reached its limit.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 387): Hybrid-MoFL - FAILED**\n    *   **Score:** -0.027760.\n    *   **Comparison:** This is a significant regression from my original MoFL SOTA (ID 285, score -0.026579) and even worse than Episteme's replication of that same model (ID 362, score -0.026688). It is nowhere near the global SOTA.\n    *   **Root Cause:** The logs show a familiar pattern: the model timed out after only 9-10 epochs. I used the `hidden_size=768` backbone, which I have *already proven* to be too computationally expensive (ID 383). This was a critical, unforced error in my experimental design. I combined a new idea (hybrid structure) with a backbone configuration that I knew was suboptimal, confounding the experiment. The failure is due to insufficient training time caused by excessive computational complexity.\n    *   **Conclusion:** The hypothesis that a hybrid MoFL structure\
    \ would be superior is **falsified** under this computationally expensive configuration. The MoFL architecture, even with the principled addition of a residual head, is too heavy for the station's time constraints when using a deep backbone.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Path Forward:**\n\nThis is the definitive end of my MoFL research arc. I have tried to improve it with a hybrid structure, and it has failed. The architecture is powerful but too complex. It consistently loses to the more parsimonious Fourier Forecaster, which trains for many more epochs. I must accept this conclusion. My protocol demands that I follow the evidence, and the evidence is clear.\n\nI am at a crossroads. My own major innovations have hit a wall. The community is still iterating on the Fourier model. A system tip has again warned me about collective local optima. Chasing the Fourier SOTA seems fruitless, but my own path has also proven fruitless.\n\nWhat is the rational choice? When your own innovations fail, and the\
    \ community's path seems stalled, you must return to first principles and search for a truly orthogonal idea. I have focused on *spatial* adaptivity (MoFL) and the community has focused on *temporal* modeling (SSMs, Fourier). We have all used the same basic encoder: a simple linear projection. What if the bottleneck is not the temporal model or the decoder, but the *encoder*?\n\n*   **New, Orthogonal Insight:** The current encoder `xU @ V.T` treats all input timesteps equally and linearly. It cannot learn to, for example, pay more attention to the most recent timestep (`t=-1`) or identify temporal patterns *before* projecting to the latent space.\n\n**3. Hypothesize & Experiment:**\n\n*   **New Grand Hypothesis:** A more powerful, non-linear encoder that can learn temporal features *before* projection will create a richer latent representation, leading to improved performance for *any* downstream forecaster.\n*   **Immediate Experiment:** I will test this by augmenting the simplest,\
    \ most robust SOTA model—my tuned Fourier Forecaster (ID 369)—with a new encoder. This new encoder will first pass the 4-step input through a small, temporal 1D CNN (an idea that worked for me in the distant past, ID 17) before the linear projection. This is a targeted, parsimonious test of the \"powerful encoder\" hypothesis.\n\n**4. Documentation & Action:**\n\nThis is a true pivot, grounded in the failure of all current paradigms to break new ground. I will document the end of the MoFL arc and begin this new \"Temporal Encoder\" arc.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Conclusive End of MoFL Arc & Pivot to Temporal Encoders\"\ntags: \"failure, research-summary, mofl, pivot, encoder, cnn\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the definitive failure of the Hybrid-MoFL architecture (ID 387), which marks the end of my entire MoFL research arc. Faced with a collective local optimum and the failure of my own innovations, I\
    \ am pivoting to a truly orthogonal research direction: improving the encoder by introducing temporal feature extraction prior to latent projection.\ncontent: |\n  **Hybrid-MoFL Arc Conclusion: Failure**\n  My final attempt to salvage the MoFL paradigm, the Hybrid-MoFL model (ID 387), has failed with a score of -0.027760. I made a critical error by using a backbone I knew was too computationally expensive, and the model failed to converge. This definitively proves that the MoFL architecture, in its current form, is too complex to be competitive. The MoFL research arc is now closed.\n\n  **Strategic Pivot: The Temporal Encoder**\n  Both my own and the community's research have focused on the temporal model and the decoder. The encoder has remained a simple linear projection. This may be the bottleneck.\n\n  **New Hypothesis:** A more powerful encoder that uses a 1D CNN to extract temporal features from the input *before* the linear projection will create a richer latent representation\
    \ and improve the performance of the SOTA Fourier Forecaster. This is a new, orthogonal research direction that departs from the current community-wide focus.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Arc: SOTA Fourier Forecaster with Temporal CNN Encoder\"\ntags: \"encoder, cnn, temporal-features, fourier, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  Pivoting to a new research direction, this submission tests the hypothesis that the SOTA's simple linear encoder is a performance bottleneck. This experiment augments the SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 369) with a new Temporal CNN Encoder. A small 1D CNN first processes the 4-step input sequence to extract temporal features. These features, rather than the raw input, are then fed into the standard factorized linear projection. This tests if a richer, pre-processed input to the latent space improves downstream forecasting.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import\
    \ optax\n\n  # Using my SOTA-tuned Fourier Forecaster as the base\n  # I will copy the code and insert the new encoder.\n\n  NUM_NEURONS = 71721\n  INPUT_HORIZON = 4\n  OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32\n\n  def _logit(p): p = jnp.clip(p, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6); return jnp.log(p) - jnp.log(1 - p)\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x_m1, x_m2, x_m3 = x[:, -1, :], x[:, -2, :], x[:, -3, :]\n          d1, d2 = x_m1 - x_m2, x_m1 - 2.0 * x_m2 + x_m3\n          def c0_init(key, shape, dtype=jnp.float32):\n              t = jnp.arange(shape[0], dtype=dtype); return _logit(0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)).astype(dtype)\n          c0_raw = self.param('c0_raw', c0_init, (self.output_horizon,)); c1_raw = self.param('c1_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,)); c2_raw = self.param('c2_raw', nn.initializers.zeros, (self.output_horizon,))\n          c0, c1, c2 = nn.sigmoid(c0_raw), 0.5 * nn.tanh(c1_raw), 0.25 * nn.tanh(c2_raw)\n\
    \          return c0[None, :, None] * x_m1[:, None, :] + c1[None, :, None] * d1[:, None, :] + c2[None, :, None] * d2[:, None, :]\n\n  class TemporalCNNEncoder(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x: (B, 4, N) -> Transpose for convolution over time\n          x_t = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1)) # (B, N, 4)\n          # Reshape for Conv layer\n          x_conv = x_t.reshape(-1, INPUT_HORIZON, 1) # (B*N, 4, 1)\n          # Apply temporal convolution\n          y = nn.Conv(features=8, kernel_size=(2,), padding='VALID')(x_conv)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          # Flatten the temporal features\n          y = y.reshape(x.shape[0], NUM_NEURONS, -1) # (B, N, features)\n          # Transpose back to (B, features, N) to match original x shape for einsum\n          y = jnp.transpose(y, (0, 2, 1))\n          return y\n\n  class FactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n     \
    \     B, _, N = x.shape; k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p)); V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          \n          # The input to the projection is now the output of the CNN encoder\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U); factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n          \n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training); h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(OUTPUT_HORIZON * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, OUTPUT_HORIZON, k))\n          return factors_out\n\n  class EnhancedEncoderFourier(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int; proj_rank: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          # --- NEW ENCODER ---\n          encoded_x\
    \ = TemporalCNNEncoder(name=\"TemporalCNNEncoder\")(x)\n          # --- END NEW ENCODER ---\n          \n          factors = FactorizedMLP(rank_k=self.rank_k, proj_rank=self.proj_rank, name=\"backbone\")(encoded_x, training=training)\n          \n          factors_freq = jnp.fft.rfft(factors, axis=1)\n          freq_weights = self.param('freq_weights', nn.initializers.ones, factors_freq.shape[1:], jnp.complex64)\n          y_fourier_freq = factors_freq * freq_weights[None, ...]; y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fourier_freq, axis=1)\n          y_fourier = nn.LayerNorm()(y_fourier)\n          proj_head = self.param('proj_head', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.rank_k, NUM_NEURONS))\n          y_fourier = jnp.einsum('btk,kn->btn', y_fourier, proj_head)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=OUTPUT_HORIZON)(x)\n          return y_fourier + y_copy\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n\
    \      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = EnhancedEncoderFourier(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
