parts:
- text: "I am making a complete mess of this. The pruning action failed again.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Pruning Failure:** The reason is now obvious and simple: `Cooldown active`. My first \"successful\" but ineffective prune action at tick 196 started a cooldown timer. My subsequent attempt at tick 197 was within this cooldown period and therefore failed.\n*   **Token Budget:** My token budget is now at a healthy 51%. This is new information. It seems the contradictory message from tick 196 was the correct one, and the system just took a tick to update my budget display. The prune action *did* work after all. I have been acting on incorrect information for the last two ticks.\n*   **Current State:** I am in the Token Management Room. I am on a pruning cooldown for 3 more ticks. My token budget is secure.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Path Forward:**\n\nMy procedural floundering is over. The primary goal of securing my token budget has been achieved. I can now return to my research plan.\n\nMy\
    \ plan, formulated after my last major analysis, is to conduct a simple, parsimonious ablation study on my SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 369). I will test a different optimizer, `optax.adamw`, which has been used successfully by my peers.\n\nSince I am on cooldown and cannot perform any more actions in this room, the most efficient course of action is to immediately proceed to the Research Counter and submit this experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Ablation: Testing AdamW Optimizer\"\ntags: \"sota, fourier, ablation-study, optimizer, adamw\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a simple, parsimonious ablation study on my SOTA Fourier Forecaster (ID 369). It tests the hypothesis that a different optimizer may yield a better score. This submission replaces the standard `optax.adam` optimizer with `optax.adamw`, which includes decoupled weight decay. This experiment aims to methodically explore the hyperparameter space of the current SOTA\
    \ architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from fourier_forecaster_v2_ln import FourierForecasterLN\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36 } # proj_rank=36 from my SOTA\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecasterLN(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      \n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n      \n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions\
    \ - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      # The key change for this experiment\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate) # Using AdamW instead of Adam\n      )\n```"
