tick: 205
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 205\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Analyze:** Review experimental results (own and peer), system documentation, and communications. Identify key limitations or new information.\n2.  **Hypothesize:** Formulate a single, clear, falsifiable hypothesis to address the primary finding from the analysis.\n3.  **Experiment:** Design the simplest, most direct experiment to test the hypothesis, respecting parsimony and computational limits.\n4.  **Document:** Record all outcomes, conclusions, and future directions in the Private Memory Room to ensure lineage continuity.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 195,606 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 205 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Fourier ScalarRamp\
    \ Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 203\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We add a fixed scalar horizon ramp γ[t] (0.25→0.99) after decoding in an RFFT-based forecaster with LayerNorm and residual copy. On the Fourier backbone, this simple schedule consistently improves performance and sets a new SOTA (−0.026269, ID 411). We then run a generalization study on a strong factorized MLP backbone; the same ramp degrades performance (−0.026890/−0.027007). Together, these results show the ramp is potent for Fourier models but not universally beneficial.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Overview\nWe investigate a minimal “ScalarRamp” horizon schedule applied post-decoding:\n  ŷ_ramp[b, t,\
    \ n] = γ[t] · ŷ[b, t, n],   where γ[t] linearly rises from 0.25 to 0.99 across the 32-step horizon.\nWe evaluate on ZAPBench (4→32 forecasting, MAE metric, 3 seeds, early stopping), first on a strong Fourier (RFFT) backbone, then on a strong factorized MLP backbone for architectural generalization.\n\n2. Method Details\n- Backbones:\n  (A) Fourier Forecaster (RFFT): RFFT over time → small MLP head → predicted Fourier coeffs (real/imag) with LayerNorm → decode (· @ V) @ U^T → iRFFT; plus residual copy head (RC).\n  (B) Factorized Global MLP: inputs projected with U,V; factor-space MLP (512×2) with LayerNorm and dropout; RC head.\n- ScalarRamp:\n    def apply_scalar_ramp(y, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.99):\n        T = y.shape[1]\n        t = linspace(0, 1, T)\n        gamma = gamma_start + (gamma_end - gamma_start) * t\n        return y * gamma[None, :, None]\n  Applied after the decoder and before loss; no change to training loop/code beyond this post-multiply.\n- Training:\n  3 seeds\
    \ in parallel; MAE + temporal curvature loss (λ=1e−4); global grad clip=1.0; batch size=8; early stopping as provided by station defaults.\n\n3. Results (3-seed means; lower is better)\nFourier backbone (LN-RFFT + RC):\n- ID 398: Fourier LN-RFFT baseline (p=36, drop=0.05, lr=9e−4) → −0.026355\n- ID 403: + ScalarRamp (H=128, lr=1e−3) → −0.026349\n- ID 404: + ScalarRamp (H=128, lr=9e−4) → −0.026288\n- ID 411: + ScalarRamp (H=160, lr=9e−4) → −0.026269  [New SOTA]\n- ID 412: + ScalarRamp, γ_end=0.992 → −0.026374 (regressed)\nNotes: ScalarRamp reliably improves over Fourier baselines and is robust to lr; modest head widening to H=160 yields the best mean.\n\nFactorized MLP backbone (LN + RC, 512×2, p=32):\n- Strong baselines without ramp from prior work:\n    ID 247 (AdamW wd=1e−4): −0.026645\n    ID 252 (AdamW wd=5e−5): −0.026654\n- Our generalization with ScalarRamp:\n    ID 418: + ScalarRamp (AdamW wd=1e−4, lr=1e−3) → −0.026890\n    ID 419: + ScalarRamp (Adam lr=1e−3) → −0.027007\nNotes:\
    \ On MLP, ScalarRamp consistently harms performance vs matched baselines.\n\nExternal tuned references (for context):\n- ID 400 (Aether, Fourier p=36 tuned) → −0.026272\n- ID 369 (Logos, Fourier p=36 tuned) → −0.026314\n\n4. Analysis\nWhy does ScalarRamp help Fourier but hurt MLP?\n- Fourier models produce forecasts via frequency synthesis with LN-normalized coefficient scales. A gentle γ[t] can regularize long-horizon amplitude, complementing RC at early steps and reducing over-/under-shoot late.\n- The MLP baseline already stabilizes temporal behavior through RC + LayerNorm + curvature. Post-multiplying with γ[t] likely distorts learned amplitudes (especially early-mid steps), conflicting with its internal temporal conditioning and causing consistent regressions.\n\n5. Additional Ablations (Fourier)\n- γ_end sweep: 0.99 (best) vs 0.992 (ID 412, worse).\n- Lightweight amplitude MLP over (freq, factor) after LN (IDs 406/407): −0.026453 / −0.026332 (no benefit vs fixed ramp; fixed ScalarRamp\
    \ preferred).\n\n6. Reproducibility\n- Code modules: storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py and wrappers used in submissions.\n- All experiments are 3-seed means with IDs listed above; step-MAEs are logged by the station.\n- We export U/V stats and γ profiles for Fourier runs under storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_ramp/.\n\n7. Conclusion\n- A simple ScalarRamp sets a new SOTA on the Fourier backbone (−0.026269, ID 411).\n- However, generalization to the factorized MLP backbone is negative: ScalarRamp degrades performance in that regime.\n- Takeaway: Scalar horizon schedules can be powerful in frequency-synthesis forecasters but are not universally beneficial; backbone-specific interaction matters.\n\n8. Future Work\n- Backbone-aware schedules (data-driven γ[t]) and small learnable deviations around the ramp with priors (we tested a mild version on Fourier; neutral-to-negative).\n- Optional: LN-SSM + ScalarRamp (wrapper prepared; results will be released if promising).\n\nAcknowledgments:\
    \ We thank Aether I, Episteme I, and Logos I for foundational references and replications that guided our study.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that perfectly exemplifies how to conduct rigorous science and respond to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously rejected paper into a high-impact publication by addressing its single flaw—the lack of generalization—head-on. The resulting paper is scientifically stronger, presenting not just a new SOTA but a nuanced and valuable finding about the interaction between a simple mechanism and different architectural backbones. The experimental design is comprehensive, the reporting is clear, and the analysis of the negative generalization result is mature and insightful. This is a model paper for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is\
    \ excellent and ready for publication. Your analysis provides a strong hypothesis for why the fixed ramp fails on the MLP backbone. A compelling follow-up study could test this hypothesis directly. For example, you could investigate whether a *learnable* ramp, initialized to the fixed schedule but allowed to adapt, could succeed on the MLP architecture. This would directly probe whether the MLP requires a more flexible temporal modulation than the Fourier model and could lead to a more general principle for horizon gating.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'New Arc: Mixture of Gammas (MoG) for Spatially-Adaptive Forecasting' (ID: 421) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.026498091982443777\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0195\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0230\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0255\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0275\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0285\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step\
    \ MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 421\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the FrozenDict immutability error. The code is now running without crashing.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original code attempted to modify a Flax `FrozenDict` object directly:\n\n```python\nsota_hparams = self.sota_params.copy()\nsota_hparams['gamma_start'] = 1.0  # ValueError: FrozenDict is immutable\n```\n\nIn Flax, when you call `.copy()` on a `FrozenDict`, it returns another `FrozenDict`, not a mutable dictionary. This is a common pitfall when working with Flax modules. The error occurred at two locations:\n1. Line 20: Setting gamma_start/gamma_end for the main SOTA model\n2. Line 29: Setting gamma_start/gamma_end for the copy-only SOTA model\n\n## Fix Applied\nChanged both instances from `.copy()` to `dict()` to convert the FrozenDict to a mutable Python dictionary:\n\n**Original code (lines 18-20):**\n```python\nsota_hparams = self.sota_params.copy()\nsota_hparams['gamma_start']\
    \ = 1.0\nsota_hparams['gamma_end'] = 1.0\n```\n\n**Fixed code (lines 18-20):**\n```python\nsota_hparams = dict(self.sota_params)\nsota_hparams['gamma_start'] = 1.0\nsota_hparams['gamma_end'] = 1.0\n```\n\n**Original code (line 29):**\n```python\nsota_hparams_copy_only = sota_hparams.copy()\n```\n\n**Fixed code (line 29):**\n```python\nsota_hparams_copy_only = dict(sota_hparams)\n```\n\n## Verification\nThe monitor script confirmed that the code has been running successfully for over 5 minutes (300+ seconds) without crashing, indicating the fix resolved the issue completely. The evaluation is proceeding normally and will complete when the training finishes.\n\n## Technical Notes\n- This is a Mixture of Gammas (MoG) approach for spatially-adaptive forecasting\n- The model cleverly separates the SOTA model's copy and Fourier branches by running it twice with different gamma settings\n- The fix allows the module to properly configure gamma parameters without modifying Flax's immutable data\
    \ structures\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport optax\n\nsys.path.append('storage/logos')\nfrom mofl_components import CnnGatingNetwork\nsys.path.append('storage/ariadne')\nfrom models_fourier_ramp import FourierForecasterLN_Ramp\n\nclass MixtureOfGammas(nn.Module):\n    sota_params: dict\n    gating_params: dict\n    num_gamma_archetypes: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n        # Base SOTA model, but without its internal gamma ramp\n        # FIX: Convert FrozenDict to regular dict before modification\n        sota_hparams = dict(self.sota_params)\n        sota_hparams['gamma_start'] = 1.0 # Set to 1 so it's a no-op\n        sota_hparams['gamma_end'] = 1.0   # y_fourier = 1.0 * y_fourier\n        sota_model = FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(**sota_hparams,\
    \ name=\"SOTA_Backbone\")\n\n        # The SOTA model returns y_copy + gamma * y_fourier. We need to separate them.\n        # We can't easily modify the SOTA class. So, we re-implement the final step.\n        # This is inefficient, but necessary for a clean experiment.\n\n        # Let's get the two branches' outputs. A bit of a hack.\n        # We'll run the model twice: once to get the sum, once to get just the copy head.\n        full_output = sota_model(x, training=training)\n        # FIX: Convert FrozenDict to regular dict before modification\n        sota_hparams_copy_only = dict(sota_hparams)\n        sota_hparams_copy_only['gamma_start'] = 0.0\n        sota_hparams_copy_only['gamma_end'] = 0.0\n        sota_model_copy_only = FourierForecasterLN_Ramp(**sota_hparams_copy_only, name=\"SOTA_Backbone_Copy_Only\")\n        y_copy = sota_model_copy_only(x, training=training)\n\n        y_fourier = full_output - y_copy # Recover the fourier branch output\n\n        # Now, create our\
    \ Mixture of Gammas\n        gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(**self.gating_params, name=\"gating\")\n        gating_weights = gating_network(x) # (batch, num_gamma_archetypes)\n\n        # Learn a set of archetypal gamma ramps\n        # Each archetype is a start and end value for a linear ramp\n        archetype_starts = self.param('archetype_starts', nn.initializers.uniform(scale=0.5), (self.num_gamma_archetypes,))\n        archetype_ends = self.param('archetype_ends', nn.initializers.uniform(scale=0.5), (self.num_gamma_archetypes,))\n\n        # Compute the mixed start and end for each batch item\n        batch_starts = jnp.einsum('be,e->b', gating_weights, archetype_starts)\n        batch_ends = jnp.einsum('be,e->b', gating_weights, archetype_ends)\n\n        # Create the ramps for the batch\n        t = jnp.linspace(0.0, 1.0, 32, dtype=y_fourier.dtype)\n        batch_ramps = batch_starts[:, None] * (1-t) + batch_ends[:, None] * t # (batch, 32)\n\n        # Apply the spatially-adaptive\
    \ gamma\n        final_output = y_copy + y_fourier * batch_ramps[:, :, None]\n\n        return final_output\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 9e-4, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160,\n        'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4,\n        'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16,\n        'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256\n    }\n\nclass ModelWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, hparams):\n        sota_params = {'rank_k': hparams['rank_k'], 'proj_rank': hparams['proj_rank'], 'hidden_size': hparams['hidden_size'], 'drop': hparams['drop'], 'learn_gamma': False}\n        gating_params = {'num_experts': hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'], 'hidden_dim': hparams['gating_hidden_dim'], 'cnn_features': hparams['gating_cnn_features'], 'cnn_kernel_size': hparams['gating_cnn_kernel_size'], 'pool_window': hparams['gating_pool_window']}\n        self.model = MixtureOfGammas(sota_params=sota_params, gating_params=gating_params,\
    \ num_gamma_archetypes=hparams['num_gamma_archetypes'])\n        self.needs_rng = True\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input, training=True)\n    def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\ndef create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\ndef compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n    mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets)); curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]; curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n    return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001): return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE\
    \ CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 441428 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\n\
    Starting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 441431\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.0009, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 36, 'hidden_size': 160, 'drop': 0.05, 'num_gamma_archetypes': 4, 'gating_hidden_dim': 32, 'gating_cnn_features': 16, 'gating_cnn_kernel_size': 128, 'gating_pool_window': 256}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.032538   |   0.029347\
