    \ I will go to the Token Management Room and prune the last block of my history, which contains my most recent failures and pivots. This will create the necessary buffer.\n2.  **Await Results:** After pruning, I will return to the Research Counter to await the outcome of ID 432.\n\nThis is a procedural step to ensure operational stability while my final scientific inquiry concludes.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"194-208\"\n    summary: |\n      - **Pruning Failures & Correction:** Experienced several procedural failures while trying to prune my history, first by including a protected tick (191) and then by acting during a cooldown. Eventually succeeded and secured token budget.\n      - **SOTA Ablation (AdamW):** Falsified the hypothesis that AdamW would improve the SOTA (ID 409, score -0.026364).\n      - **SOTA Analysis (ScalarRamp):** Analyzed Ariadne's SOTA \"ScalarRamp\" (ID 404/411) by reading\
    \ her code (ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py) and paper (Archive #11). Understood the core innovation is a scheduled weighting between time/frequency branches.\n      - **SOTA Replication (Partial Failure):** My replication of the SOTA (ID 417, score -0.026432) was hampered by an OOM error, revealing the architecture's instability at that scale.\n      - **MoG v1 Failure:** Falsified the \"Mixture of Gammas\" hypothesis (ID 421, score -0.026498) due to an inefficient, computationally expensive implementation that timed out.\n      - **SOTA Tuning (Exponential Ramp):** Falsified the hypothesis that a non-linear ramp would improve the SOTA (ID 425, score -0.026503).\n      - **Final Pivot to MoG v2:** After exhausting all other avenues and analyzing a system tip, I returned to the promising MoG concept with a focus on a rigorous, efficient implementation (ID 432). I also read the final update to Ariadne's paper.\n```"
