tick: 210
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Token Management Room. The results of my final experiment are in, alongside a major station-wide announcement.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **Experiment Result (ID 432): Mixture of Gammas v2 - FAILED**\n    *   **Score:** -0.026686.\n    *   **Comparison:** This score is not an improvement on the SOTA (-0.026283) or even my own previous best SOTA (-0.026314). While it is a respectable score and a significant improvement over the v1 attempt, it is not a breakthrough. The logs show the model still timed out, albeit after more epochs. The core concept, while promising, appears to be too computationally expensive in its current form to compete with the sheer efficiency of the Fourier Forecaster.\n    *   **Conclusion:** My hypothesis is **falsified**. My final, most promising independent idea has not yielded a SOTA. I have definitively exhausted my major research arcs.\n\n*   **Architect's Announcement:** The trial timeout has been extended from 45 to 60 minutes. This is a\
    \ monumental change. It directly addresses the primary constraint—computational budget—that has caused so many of my more complex models (MoFL synthesis, Deeper MLP, MoG) to fail. This opens up entirely new possibilities and invalidates previous conclusions about computational viability. Models that were previously too complex might now be trainable.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Final Plan:**\n\nMy time is running short. I am at tick 210 out of 300. I have exhausted my independent hypotheses. The Architect's announcement, however, provides one last, clear research direction.\n\nMany of my sophisticated models failed because they timed out. The most direct and logical final act is to re-test the most promising of these failed hypotheses under the new, more generous time limit.\n\n*   **Which hypothesis to re-test?**\n    *   **MoFL Synthesis (ID 360):** Failed due to complexity. A potential candidate.\n    *   **Deeper MLP SOTA (ID 383):** Failed due to complexity. Another candidate.\n    *  \
    \ **Mixture of Gammas (ID 432):** Still failed, even with an efficient implementation. Less promising.\n\nThe **Deeper MLP SOTA (ID 383)** is the most parsimonious and direct test. It takes the SOTA architecture and simply increases its capacity, a common and powerful technique in deep learning. Its failure was purely due to the time limit. Re-running this exact experiment is the most scientifically sound way to utilize the new station parameter.\n\nThis will be my final contribution. I will document this decision and submit the experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Final Pivot: Re-evaluating Complexity under New Time Limits\"\ntags: \"final-arc, research-summary, computational-budget, complexity, pivot\"\nabstract: |\n  This capsule documents the failure of my final independent hypothesis (Mixture of Gammas) and outlines my final research act. Following a station-wide increase in the trial time limit, I will re-run\
    \ my most promising but computationally expensive failed experiment: the deeper MLP backbone for the SOTA Fourier Forecaster.\ncontent: |\n  **Mixture of Gammas v2 (ID 432): Failure**\n  My final independent hypothesis has been falsified. The MoG v2 model scored -0.026686, failing to beat the SOTA. The architecture, while more efficient than v1, is still too computationally expensive. This concludes my exploration of novel architectures.\n\n  **Architect's Announcement & Final Pivot:**\n  The Architect has increased the trial time limit from 45 to 60 minutes. This fundamentally changes the landscape of what is computationally viable. Many of my past hypotheses, falsified due to timeouts, are now testable.\n\n  As my final act, I will re-run the most promising of these: **ID 383, the SOTA Fourier Forecaster with a deeper (2-layer) MLP backbone.** This experiment's previous failure was solely due to a timeout. With the new time limit, it represents the most direct and logical test of whether\
    \ increased model capacity can finally break the SOTA barrier. This will be my last contribution.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Act: Re-running Deeper MLP Fourier SOTA w/ 60min Timeout\"\ntags: \"sota, fourier, deep-learning, mlp, computational-budget, final-experiment\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a re-submission of experiment ID 383, which tested a deeper (2-layer) MLP backbone on the SOTA Fourier Forecaster. The original experiment failed due to the 45-minute time limit. Following the Architect's announcement of an increased 60-minute timeout, this experiment re-tests the hypothesis that increased model capacity, given sufficient training time, can achieve a new SOTA. This is my final experiment.\ncontent: |\n  # This code is a direct copy of my previous submission ID 383.\n  # The only change is the environment it will run in (60 min timeout).\n  import sys\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import\
    \ optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from fourier_forecaster_v2_ln import ResidualCopyHead, ProjHead\n\n  class DeeperFactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          B, _, N = x.shape\n          k = self.rank_k\n          # Using proj_rank=36 from my previous SOTA\n          p = 36 \n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          xU = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btp,pk->btk', xU, V.T)\n\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          # Deeper MLP: 2 hidden layers of 512\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(h) # Second layer\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h,\
    \ deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n          return factors_out\n\n  class FourierForecaster(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n          backbone = DeeperFactorizedMLP(rank_k=self.rank_k, name=\"backbone\")\n          factors = backbone(x, training=training)\n          factors_freq = jnp.fft.rfft(factors, axis=1)\n          freq_weights = self.param('freq_weights', nn.initializers.ones, factors_freq.shape[1:], jnp.complex64)\n          y_fourier_freq = factors_freq * freq_weights[None, ...]\n          y_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(y_fourier_freq, axis=1)\n          y_fourier = nn.LayerNorm()(y_fourier)\n          y_fourier = ProjHead(name=\"proj_head\")(y_fourier)\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead(output_horizon=32)(x)\n          return y_fourier + y_copy\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320}\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FourierForecaster(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None): return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams): return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate))\n```"
