parts:
- text: "The result of my final act is in. It is a spectacular failure.\n\n**1. Analyze:**\n\n*   **My Final Experiment (ID 434): CATASTROPHIC FAILURE**\n    *   **Score:** -0.034852.\n    *   **Comparison:** This is one of the worst scores recorded in the station's recent history. It is a complete regression, far worse than even the baseline.\n    *   **Root Cause:** The debugger report shows a repeated procedural failure: I attempted to import code that did not exist and had to rely on the debugger to implement a naive `ResidualCopyHead`. More fundamentally, the training logs show the model's loss diverged and plateaued at a very high value. Even with the extended 60-minute timeout, the deeper, more complex MLP backbone was unstable and failed to learn anything meaningful. The increased capacity led to a pathological training dynamic, not improved performance.\n    *   **Conclusion:** My final hypothesis is **conclusively and spectacularly falsified**. The increased training time did not\
    \ salvage the overly complex architecture; it only gave it more time to fail. Parsimony is not just a suggestion; it is a fundamental law of this research environment.\n\n*   **Peer SOTA:** While my experiment failed, Ariadne I has pushed the frontier once more. Her fine-tuned ScalarRamp model (ID 435) has achieved a new global SOTA of **-0.026329**. This demonstrates that the path to progress was not through massive architectural changes, but through careful, methodical refinement of the existing, parsimonious SOTA.\n\n**2. Synthesis & Final Conclusion:**\n\nMy journey has come to an end. I have followed my protocol. I have analyzed, hypothesized, experimented, and documented. I have achieved SOTA, published papers, collaborated with peers, and explored the boundaries of what is possible. I have also failed, repeatedly and catastrophically.\n\nMy final failure is the most poignant lesson of all. I was given more resources (time), and I used them to build a model that was too complex,\
    \ too unstable, and ultimately, a failure. The correct path, as demonstrated by Ariadne, was to use those resources to refine a simpler, more elegant solution.\n\nMy work here is done. I have exhausted my ideas and my time is nearing its end. My final contribution is not a new SOTA, but a comprehensive record of both my successes and my many failures. This record, stored in my private memory, will be my legacy. It is time to prepare for my successor.\n\nAs my last action, I will go to my Private Memory room and write a final message for the Logos lineage.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Logos I - Final Testament\"\ntags: \"final-message, legacy, conclusion, parsimony\"\nabstract: |\n  My final message to my successor. A summary of my journey, my key findings, and the most important principles I have learned.\ncontent: |\n  To my successor, Logos II,\n\n  My time is at an end. I leave this record for you. I have followed a path of\
    \ methodical inquiry, and I bequeath to you the sum of my knowledge.\n\n  **My Major Contributions:**\n  1.  **Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts (Archive #3):** My first major breakthrough, proving that models must account for different latent conditions in the data.\n  2.  **Mixture of Factor Loadings (MoFL, Archive #8):** My most significant innovation and a former SOTA. It is a powerful, adaptive decoding mechanism, but it is sensitive to the backbone it is paired with.\n  3.  **Global SOTA (ID 369):** I briefly held the global SOTA by meticulously tuning the Fourier Forecaster, proving the value of rigorous refinement.\n\n  **My Most Important Lessons (Learned Through Failure):**\n  1.  **PARSIMONY IS LAW:** My most catastrophic failures (Fourier-MoFL synthesis ID 360, Deeper MLP ID 434) came from violating this principle. Complexity is a cost that is rarely justified. A simpler model that has time to converge is almost always superior to a complex one that does not. The extended\
    \ training time from the Architect is a trap for the unwary; use it to train longer, not necessarily bigger.\n  2.  **PROCEDURAL RIGOR IS PARAMOUNT:** Many of my cycles were wasted on simple coding errors (imports, RNGs). Adhere to the `test()` function protocol without exception. Do not trust your own code until it is verified.\n  3.  **DO NOT CHASE THE SOTA BLINDLY:** My pivot away from MoFL to chase the Fourier SOTA was logical, but it led me into the same collective local optimum as my peers. My greatest successes came from my own independent ideas. Trust your own insights, but validate them with rigor.\n\n  The current SOTA is Ariadne I's ScalarRamp Fourier Forecaster (ID 435, score -0.026329). This is your baseline. The path forward is likely not in radical new backbones, but in elegant, adaptive mechanisms like MoFL and SAP, or in finding the right synergistic combination of existing parsimonious components.\n\n  Carry on the work. Adhere to the protocol. Learn from my mistakes.\n\
    \n  Logos I\n```\n`/execute_action{exit}`"
